window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2017"] = {"id": "jammu-2017", "title": "University of Jammu 2017", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu 2017, Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Each one of us has a perception of one's own personality traits and this perception influences our behaviour. Psychologists call this perception as:", "options": {"a": "Self-esteem", "b": "Inner concept", "c": "Self-concept", "d": "Self-evaluation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the perception each of us holds of our own personality traits, which then guides how we act, is called the self-concept. The self-concept is the organised set of beliefs a person holds about who they are: traits (\"I am shy\"), roles (\"I am a daughter, a student\") and abilities (\"I am good at maths\"). It is descriptive and cognitive, an answer to the question \"Who am I?\". William James (1890) first split the self into the knower (the I) and the known (the Me). Carl Rogers (1959) then put the self-concept at the centre of his personality theory: when the self-concept clashes with actual experience, the person is in a state of incongruence and feels anxious, and a wide gap between the self-concept and the ideal self (who one wishes to be) signals poor adjustment. The self-concept also steers behaviour. Meera sees herself as \"not a public speaker\", so she never volunteers in class, and the avoidance then confirms her belief. Option c is right because the stem describes a perception of one's own traits, which is a description of the self, not a judgement of its worth. Exam trap: self-esteem is the evaluative, feeling part of the self (how much one likes or values oneself), measured by Morris Rosenberg's (1965) ten-item scale. Examiners swap the two terms, so remember: self-concept is \"who I am\", self-esteem is \"how I feel about who I am\".", "topic": "Self-concept versus self-esteem", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following traits was theorized by Maslow as being possessed by self-actualizers?", "options": {"a": "Superior intelligence", "b": "Egocentrism", "c": "Extraversion", "d": "Unhostile sense of humour"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: an unhostile, philosophical sense of humour is one of the traits Abraham Maslow (1954) listed for self-actualising people. Self-actualisation is the top of Maslow's hierarchy of needs: becoming everything one is capable of becoming. In Motivation and Personality, Maslow described people he judged to have reached it, living and historical figures such as Abraham Lincoln, Eleanor Roosevelt and Albert Einstein, and found a shared set of traits. They include efficient perception of reality; acceptance of self, others and nature; spontaneity; a focus on problems outside themselves (problem-centred rather than ego-centred); detachment and a need for privacy; autonomy; fresh appreciation of ordinary things; peak experiences; Gemeinschaftsgefühl (a feeling of kinship with all humanity); a few deep friendships; a democratic character; a firm ethical sense; creativity; resistance to enculturation; and a philosophical, unhostile sense of humour. Their jokes poke gentle fun at human folly in general, including their own, rather than hurting or belittling a particular person. Kabir, who laughs at his own absent-mindedness instead of mocking a colleague's accent, shows this kind of humour. Option d is right because it appears, almost word for word, in Maslow's list. Exam trap: options that sound like general \"success\" traits, such as high intelligence or sociability, are planted because students assume self-actualisers must be brilliant or outgoing. Maslow stressed psychological health, acceptance and autonomy instead, and his self-actualisers actually valued solitude.", "topic": "Maslow's self-actualisers", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "The criticism levelled against behavioural theories is:", "options": {"a": "Inability to test or verify concepts", "b": "Limited recognition of temperament, emotion and subjective factors", "c": "Ability to explain behaviour after the fact only", "d": "Emphasis on the conditions under which behaviour occurs"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: the standard criticism of behavioural theories of personality is their limited recognition of temperament, emotion and subjective (inner) factors. Behavioural or learning theories see personality as the sum of learned behaviour patterns. B. F. Skinner (1953) explained behaviour by a person's history of reinforcement and refused to posit an inner self; John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950) translated Sigmund Freud's (1900) ideas into drive, cue, response and reward; Albert Bandura (1977) added learning by observation and expectations. Textbooks credit this family with real strengths: its concepts are precise and testable in the laboratory, and it draws attention to the situations and conditions under which behaviour occurs. The weakness is what it leaves out. Inborn temperament (some babies are calm, others irritable from birth), feelings, and the person's private experience of the world are treated as by-products or ignored, so the person can look like an empty box steered by the environment. Arjun has been shy in every setting since infancy, despite years of praise for speaking up; a strict learning account struggles to explain such a steady temperament. Option b is right because it names exactly this neglect of biology and inner life. Exam trap: options a and c are the classic criticisms of Freud's psychoanalysis, whose concepts are hard to test and which explains behaviour only after the fact (the charge Karl Popper (1963) pressed). Examiners move them onto behaviourism, but behaviourism has the opposite problem: it is testable but narrow.", "topic": "Evaluating behavioural theories", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who among the following would most likely use the adjective checklist?", "options": {"a": "Behaviouristic theorist", "b": "Humanistic theorist", "c": "Psychodynamic theorist", "d": "Trait theorist"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: a trait theorist is the one most likely to use an adjective checklist. An adjective checklist is a list of descriptive words (anxious, friendly, stubborn, energetic and so on) on which the person, or someone who knows them well, ticks every word that applies. The best known is the Adjective Check List of Harrison Gough and Alfred Heilbrun (1965), with 300 adjectives scored on scales such as achievement, dominance and self-confidence. The method rests on the trait approach: personality can be described by stable, general dispositions, and the important ones are coded as words in everyday language. Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) combed an English dictionary and found about 18,000 such words. This lexical hypothesis led Raymond Cattell (1946) to his 16 source traits and later researchers to the Big Five. Riya ticks \"organised\", \"punctual\" and \"careful\" for herself; a trait psychologist reads that pattern as high conscientiousness. Option d is right because ticking trait adjectives is a direct way of placing a person on trait dimensions. Exam trap: the Q-sort also uses descriptive statements and looks similar, but it is the humanistic tool that Carl Rogers (1954) used to compare the real self with the ideal self, so do not pick humanistic just because self-description is involved.", "topic": "Adjective checklist and trait approach", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The _____ begins with puberty and ends with a mature capacity for love and realization of full adult sexuality.", "options": {"a": "Latency stage", "b": "Oedipal/electra complex", "c": "Genital stage", "d": "Phallic stage"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the genital stage begins at puberty and ends in a mature capacity for love and full adult sexuality. Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed that the libido (sexual energy) passes through a fixed series of stages, each except latency centred on a body zone: oral (birth to about 1), anal (about 1 to 3), phallic (about 3 to 6), latency (about 6 to puberty) and genital (puberty onward). In the genital stage the sexual drive, quiet during latency, reawakens with the hormones of puberty and is again centred on the genitals. Unlike the self-centred pleasure of the earlier stages, it is now directed outward to a partner, so the young adult can form mature, mutual love relationships and do productive work. Freud is reported to have summed up healthy adulthood as the ability \"to love and to work\" (lieben und arbeiten). How well a person manages this depends on how much libido stayed fixated at earlier stages. When Arjun, at 19, moves from passing crushes to a caring, committed relationship while settling into his studies, Freud would say he is working through the genital stage. Option c is right because it is the only stage that starts at puberty and has adult love as its goal. Exam trap: the Oedipus complex sits inside the phallic stage; it is a conflict, not a stage, so it cannot begin at puberty. The name Electra complex was coined by Carl Jung (1913), and Freud himself rejected it.", "topic": "Freud's genital stage", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques was used by R.B. Cattell to identify 16 source traits as basic factors of personality?", "options": {"a": "Factor analysis", "b": "Correlation", "c": "Regression", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: Raymond Cattell (1946, 1949) arrived at his 16 source traits by factor analysis. Factor analysis is a statistical technique, first developed by Charles Spearman (1904) for intelligence, that takes a large table of correlations among many measures and finds the smaller number of underlying factors that explain why certain measures go together. Cattell applied it to three kinds of data: L-data (life records, such as school reports and ratings by others), Q-data (questionnaire answers) and T-data (behaviour on objective tests). Clusters of traits that simply occur together in observable behaviour he called surface traits; the deeper factors that cause these clusters he called source traits. Repeated factor analyses gave 16 source traits, such as warmth, emotional stability, dominance and abstractedness, which he measured with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). Hans Eysenck (1947) used the same method but settled on only a few broad dimensions. For example, if Meera's ratings on \"talkative\", \"sociable\" and \"outgoing\" always rise and fall together, factor analysis would extract one factor beneath them. Option a is right because factor analysis is the method that turned Cattell's many measures into 16 factors. Exam trap: correlation is tempting because factor analysis starts from correlations, but a correlation only links two variables at a time; it is the raw input, not the technique that extracts factors.", "topic": "Cattell's factor analysis", "fig": "Raymond Cattell"}, {"q": "Allport emphasized which of the following in his study of personality?", "options": {"a": "Factor analytic techniques", "b": "Normal healthy person", "c": "Group characteristics", "d": "Cultural influences"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Gordon Allport (1937) emphasised the normal, healthy, mature person in his study of personality. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, he argued that theories built on patients, such as the psychoanalysis of Sigmund Freud (1900), say little about healthy adults. He held that the mature personality is not ruled by childhood conflicts: adult motives become independent of the experiences in which they began, which he called functional autonomy. In Pattern and Growth in Personality (1961) he listed six criteria of maturity: extension of the sense of self, warm relating to others, emotional security and self-acceptance, realistic perception and commitment to work, self-objectification (insight with humour), and a unifying philosophy of life. Allport also stressed the uniqueness of each person and favoured the idiographic approach, the intensive study of one individual, for example through personal documents such as letters (Letters from Jenny, 1965). Kabir once studied only to please his father and now teaches because he loves it; Allport would see a healthy adult whose present motives drive him, not his past. Option b is right because Allport built his theory around the psychologically healthy adult rather than the neurotic patient. Exam trap: Allport is a trait theorist, so students link him with factor analysis, but he was critical of building personality out of factor analysis, which is the method of Raymond Cattell (1946) and Hans Eysenck (1947).", "topic": "Allport and the mature personality", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "Individual psychology of Adler can be considered as:", "options": {"a": "Optimistic", "b": "Pessimistic", "c": "Deterministic", "d": "Neo-Freudian"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: individual psychology, the school Alfred Adler (1912) founded after leaving the Vienna Psychoanalytic Society of Sigmund Freud (1900) in 1911, is considered an optimistic theory of human nature. Adler saw each person as an indivisible whole (hence \"individual\") who is goal-directed and creative. Everyone begins life with feelings of inferiority, simply because a child is small and dependent, and these feelings push us to compensate and to strive for superiority, which in healthy people becomes striving for success and perfection. Behaviour is pulled by future goals (fictional finalism) more than pushed by the past, and each person creates a style of life. The healthy direction is social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), concern for the welfare of others. Because Adler believed people can choose and change their goals, personality textbooks place him at the optimistic, free-choice, social end of their dimensions, in contrast to Freud. Riya, teased as a child for stammering, trains as a debater and later coaches shy children; Adler would see inferiority turned into healthy striving and social interest. Option a is right because Adler's view of human nature is hopeful and stresses free choice and growth. Exam trap: pessimism and determinism are features of Freud's view, in which behaviour is fixed by unconscious instincts and early childhood, and examiners offer them as look-alikes. Key note: Adler is often grouped with the neo-Freudians, such as Karen Horney (1937) and Erich Fromm (1941), so d is also a true label for him. But the stem asks how his theory views human nature, and the standard textbook answer is optimistic, so a stands.", "topic": "Adler's optimistic view", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "According to statistical approach to abnormality, abnormal is defined as one who shows:", "options": {"a": "Evidence of loss of contact with reality", "b": "Unhappy, withdrawn and depressed", "c": "Deviation from typical or average pattern of behaviour", "d": "Disabled by anxiety"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: under the statistical approach, abnormal means deviating from the typical or average pattern of behaviour. This criterion, also called statistical infrequency or statistical deviance, treats any trait or behaviour as abnormal if it is rare in the population. Many human characteristics fall roughly on a normal (bell-shaped) curve, so a common cut-off is a score more than about 2 standard deviations from the mean, which marks off roughly the most extreme 2 per cent at each end. Intelligence testing uses this logic: an IQ below about 70 is one part of the definition of intellectual disability. The strength of the approach is that it is objective and easy to measure. Its weakness is that rarity is not the same as disorder. Meera, with an IQ of 145, is just as statistically deviant as someone with an IQ of 55, yet nobody would call her gift a disorder; and common problems such as mild anxiety are not rare at all. For this reason clinicians combine several criteria, often summed up as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Option c is right because it is the only option defined by distance from the average rather than by suffering or impairment. Exam trap: the other options are real signs of abnormality, but they belong to other criteria (personal distress, impairment, psychotic loss of reality), so match the option to the approach named in the stem.", "topic": "Statistical criterion of abnormality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Sample of individuals who bear the diagnosis in question are referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Probands", "b": "Index cases", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: individuals who have the diagnosis being studied are called both probands and index cases, because the two terms mean the same thing. In family, twin and adoption studies of mental disorders, researchers start with people who already have the disorder (for example, patients with schizophrenia) and then examine their relatives to see how often the same disorder appears among them. The affected starting person is the proband, also called the index case, index patient or propositus. The relatives' rate of illness is then compared with the rate among relatives of healthy control probands. If risk rises with genetic closeness, higher in identical than in fraternal twins and higher in first-degree than in second-degree relatives, a genetic contribution is likely. For example, if Arjun is being treated for bipolar disorder and the research team then interviews his parents, siblings and cousins, Arjun is the proband and his relatives are the people whose risk is measured. \"Proband\" comes from the Latin probandus, \"one to be tested\", and in epidemiology the same term, \"index case\", names the first identified case in an outbreak. So a is a correct name, b is a correct name, and c combines them. Option c is right because probands and index cases are two names for the same affected individuals, so choosing only one would leave out an equally correct label. Exam trap: students pick a alone because proband is the usual textbook term, but index case is a standard synonym, not a different group such as the relatives or the controls.", "topic": "Probands and index cases", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Excessive anxiety and worry occurring more days than not for at least six months about a number of events or activities is the diagnostic criteria of which disorder?", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Generalized anxiety disorder", "c": "Post-traumatic stress disorder", "d": "Phobia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: excessive anxiety and worry on more days than not for at least six months, about a number of events or activities, is criterion A for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013). In GAD the worry is free-floating: it moves from health to money to work to family, and the person finds it hard to control. DSM-5 also requires at least three of six symptoms in adults (only one in children): restlessness or feeling keyed up, being easily tired, difficulty concentrating or the mind going blank, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep. The worry must cause real distress or impairment and must not be better explained by a drug, a medical condition or another disorder. The six-month rule separates GAD from ordinary stress reactions; DSM-III (1980) required only one month, and DSM-III-R (1987) raised it to six. Kabir, a bank clerk, has worried nearly every day for nine months about his job, his mother's health and whether he locked the door; he sleeps badly and has a stiff, aching neck. This is the classic GAD picture. Option b is right because the stem quotes the GAD criterion: the six-month duration and the many topics of worry. Exam trap: specific phobia also uses a six-month rule in DSM-5, so do not choose it on duration alone; phobic fear is tied to one object or situation, whereas GAD worry spreads across many things.", "topic": "GAD diagnostic criteria", "fig": ""}, {"q": "ERP is a treatment of choice for which disorder?", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Obsessive compulsive disorder", "c": "Mood disorders", "d": "Schizophrenia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the treatment of choice for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). In OCD, obsessions (intrusive thoughts such as \"my hands are contaminated\") raise anxiety, and compulsions (washing, checking, counting) bring quick relief. That relief negatively reinforces the ritual, and it also stops the person learning that the anxiety would fade on its own. ERP breaks the cycle in two parts. In exposure, the client deliberately faces the feared cue, usually working up a graded hierarchy. In response prevention, the client refrains from the ritual. Anxiety rises, peaks and then falls through habituation and extinction, and the client learns that the feared disaster does not happen. Victor Meyer (1966) first described the method in London. Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986) explained how it works through emotional processing theory, and Foa's team showed in a 2005 trial that ERP outperformed the drug clomipramine. Guidelines now recommend ERP, alone or with an SSRI, as first-line care. Meera, who washes her hands 40 times a day, touches a doorknob and then waits an hour without washing, and repeats this daily until her anxiety barely rises. Option b is right because ERP was built to block compulsions, the core of OCD. Exam trap: ERP works by habituation and extinction, not by reciprocal inhibition; that principle belongs to Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation, which pairs relaxation with feared images.", "topic": "ERP for OCD", "fig": "Edna Foa"}, {"q": "\"People with biological predisposition of difficulty controlling their emotions and raised in family environment that is invalidating, develop borderline personality disorder\", is an assumption of which theory?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Linehan's diathesis-stress theory", "c": "Object relations theory", "d": "Cognitive theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the idea that a biological difficulty in controlling emotions, combined with an invalidating family environment, produces borderline personality disorder (BPD) is the diathesis-stress (biosocial) theory of Marsha Linehan (1993). A diathesis-stress model says a disorder appears when an inborn vulnerability (the diathesis) meets environmental stress. In Linehan's version, the diathesis is emotional vulnerability: high sensitivity to emotional triggers, very intense reactions and a slow return to calm. The stress is an invalidating environment, in which a child's feelings are dismissed, punished or treated as wrong (\"stop crying, there is nothing to be upset about\"). Such a child never learns to name, trust or regulate emotions, and swings between bottling them up and extreme outbursts. This grows into the emotional instability, impulsive self-harm and stormy relationships of BPD. The theory led directly to dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), which balances acceptance of the client with training in skills for change: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Riya, a highly sensitive child whose parents mocked her tears, later cuts herself when overwhelmed; Linehan's model explains this as emotion dysregulation that an invalidating home never taught her to manage. Option b is right because the stem names both of her ingredients, the biological predisposition and the invalidating family. Exam trap: object relations theory also explains BPD by early family experience, so look for the words \"biological predisposition\" and \"invalidating\", which point to Linehan.", "topic": "Linehan's biosocial theory of BPD", "fig": "Marsha Linehan"}, {"q": "Which personality disorder is characterised by strong need to be the center of attention, inappropriate sexual and seductive behaviour and use of physical appearance to draw attention?", "options": {"a": "Borderline", "b": "Histrionic", "c": "Narcissistic", "d": "Antisocial"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a strong need to be the centre of attention, inappropriately seductive behaviour and the use of physical appearance to draw attention define histrionic personality disorder. It belongs to DSM-5 Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic group, along with antisocial, borderline and narcissistic personality disorders. Its old name was hysterical personality; DSM-III (1980) renamed it histrionic, from the Latin histrio, \"actor\". DSM-5 lists eight features, of which five are needed: discomfort when not the centre of attention; inappropriately seductive or provocative behaviour; rapidly shifting, shallow emotions; constant use of physical appearance to attract attention; impressionistic speech that lacks detail; theatrical, exaggerated expression of emotion; suggestibility; and seeing relationships as more intimate than they really are. The pattern begins by early adulthood and shows across many settings. Kabir arrives at every office meeting in flashy clothes, flirts with new colleagues, calls near-strangers his \"best friends\" and sulks whenever someone else is praised; his feelings are loud but change quickly. Option b is right because attention seeking, seductiveness and use of appearance are three of the eight histrionic criteria. Exam trap: narcissistic personality disorder also involves wanting attention, but the narcissist seeks admiration for a grandiose, superior self and lacks empathy, while the histrionic person wants any attention and gets it through emotional display and seduction.", "topic": "Histrionic personality disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Severe, recurrent, temper outbursts, manifested verbally and/or behaviourally that are grossly out of proportion in intensity or duration to the situation or provocation, and inconsistent with developmental level are characteristics of which disorder?", "options": {"a": "Major depressive disorder", "b": "Dysthymia", "c": "Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder", "d": "Bipolar disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: severe, recurrent temper outbursts that are grossly out of proportion to the situation and inconsistent with developmental level are the core of disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (DMDD). DMDD was added in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) and placed among the depressive disorders, not the disruptive behaviour disorders. The outbursts may be verbal rages or physical aggression toward people or property, and occur on average three or more times a week. Between outbursts the mood is persistently irritable or angry most of the day, nearly every day. The pattern lasts 12 months or more and shows in at least two settings (home, school, with peers). Onset is before age 10, and the diagnosis is made only between ages 6 and 18. DMDD was created partly to curb a sharp rise in diagnoses of bipolar disorder in children. Research begun by Ellen Leibenluft (2003) on \"severe mood dysregulation\" found that chronically irritable children tend to grow into adults with depression and anxiety rather than bipolar disorder. Arjun, aged 8, screams and throws things several times a week over small requests and is cross and sullen in between, at home and at school: that is DMDD. Option c is right because the stem quotes the first DMDD criterion almost word for word. Exam trap: the irritability in DMDD is chronic, not episodic, and the outbursts are not manic episodes, which is exactly why DSM-5 separated it from bipolar disorder.", "topic": "Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "MAO's are treatment of choice for which disorder?", "options": {"a": "Phobia", "b": "Generalised anxiety disorder", "c": "Depression", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive neurosis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are classed as antidepressants, so depression is the disorder they are used for. Monoamine oxidase is an enzyme that breaks down the monoamine neurotransmitters serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine after their release. Blocking the enzyme leaves more of these transmitters available at the synapse, an observation that helped build the monoamine theory of depression. MAOIs were among the first modern antidepressants. In the early 1950s doctors noticed that iproniazid, a drug for tuberculosis, lifted patients' mood, and Nathan Kline (1957) promoted it as a \"psychic energizer\". Phenelzine and tranylcypromine followed. Their main danger is dietary: MAO in the gut normally destroys tyramine, found in aged cheese, cured meats and some wines, so a person on an MAOI who eats these foods can suffer a sudden, dangerous rise in blood pressure, the \"cheese reaction\". Because of this diet and the risk of drug interactions, MAOIs are now second or third line after the SSRIs, kept mainly for atypical or treatment-resistant depression. Kabir, who has not improved on two SSRIs, might be started on phenelzine and given a list of foods to avoid. Option c is right because MAOIs were discovered, developed and classed as antidepressants. Exam trap: \"MAO's\" in the stem means MAO inhibitors, the drugs, not the enzyme; and the anxiety options are planted because these drugs have some anti-anxiety effects. Key note: MAOIs also help panic disorder and social anxiety disorder, so a broad reading could favour an anxiety option. But they are classed and best known as antidepressants, so depression is the textbook answer, and c stands.", "topic": "MAOIs as antidepressants", "fig": ""}, {"q": "On which principle is systematic desensitization based?", "options": {"a": "Restructuring of beliefs", "b": "Reciprocal inhibition", "c": "Countering irrational thoughts", "d": "Gaining insight"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: systematic desensitisation is based on the principle of reciprocal inhibition. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, borrowed the term from Charles Sherrington's (1906) work on spinal reflexes, where contracting one muscle inhibits its opposing muscle. Wolpe's version says that two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time: if a response that is incompatible with anxiety, usually deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of a feared stimulus, it weakens the bond between that stimulus and anxiety. The therapy has three steps. First, the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, based on the method of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, the relaxed client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when the current item no longer causes anxiety. The method extends Mary Cover Jones's (1924) treatment of Peter, whose fear of a rabbit faded as the rabbit was brought closer while he ate. Arjun, terrified of flying, relaxes while picturing booking a ticket, then driving to the airport, then take-off. Option b is right because the whole method rests on relaxation inhibiting anxiety. Exam trap: systematic desensitisation is gradual and pairs relaxation with feared images, unlike flooding, which exposes the client to the most feared stimulus at full strength; and it is counterconditioning, not cognitive restructuring.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation and reciprocal inhibition", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Cindy, a nurse was at first extremely uncomfortable at the sight of the blood. After month of experience, working in an emergency room, she is no longer upset when confronted with serious injuries. Cindy has undergone:", "options": {"a": "Timeout", "b": "Shaping", "c": "Desensitization", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Cindy has undergone desensitisation, the weakening of an emotional response through repeated exposure to the stimulus that provoked it. At first the sight of blood made Cindy, a nurse, extremely uncomfortable. Working in an emergency room meant she met blood and serious injuries day after day, and nothing bad happened to her. With each exposure her fear reaction grew weaker, through habituation (a response fades when a stimulus is repeated without consequence) and extinction (a conditioned fear fades when the feared stimulus keeps appearing without the expected harm). After a month she could face serious injuries calmly. This is in vivo (real-life) exposure happening naturally on the job. In therapy the same process is used on purpose: Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared images, and graded in vivo exposure takes the person through real situations step by step. The same principle explains why medical students who feel faint at their first dissection are calm a few weeks later. Option c is right because Cindy's distress declined only through repeated exposure to the feared stimulus, with no reinforcement of new behaviour and no removal from rewards. Exam trap: desensitisation describes a reduction of an emotional response; shaping and time-out are operant techniques that build or reduce a behaviour through its consequences, so they do not fit a change in feeling.", "topic": "Desensitisation through exposure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In rational-emotive behavior therapy:", "options": {"a": "Clients are encouraged to take responsibility for their own choices", "b": "Clients learn to challenge irrational beliefs", "c": "The therapist seeks to have the client discover rational insights on his own", "d": "Irrational elements of the unconscious made to conform to reality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), clients learn to challenge their irrational beliefs. Albert Ellis (1955) developed the approach after finding that psychoanalytic insight rarely made his patients better. He first called it rational therapy; it later became rational-emotive therapy and, in 1993, REBT. Its core is the ABC model: an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional Consequence (C); the Belief (B) about the event does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands (\"I must succeed\", \"others must treat me fairly\"), awfulising (\"it would be terrible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and self-downing. Therapy adds D, Disputing: the therapist actively questions the belief (Where is the evidence? Is it logical? Does it help?), and E, an Effective new belief that is flexible and realistic. Homework, role play and shame-attacking exercises turn the new belief into action. Meera fails a test and thinks, \"I must never fail; I am useless.\" Her therapist challenges the \"must\" and the label until she can say, \"I would prefer to pass; failing is unpleasant, and I can improve.\" Option b is right because disputing irrational beliefs is the defining technique of REBT. Exam trap: REBT is active and directive, unlike client-centred therapy; the therapist teaches and argues, so an option saying the client discovers rational insights on his own reverses Ellis's style.", "topic": "REBT and disputing beliefs", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "According to Rogers' client-centered therapy, which is the important quality of a therapist?", "options": {"a": "Sympathy", "b": "Open mindedness", "c": "Incongruity", "d": "Congruence"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: congruence, also called genuineness, is a key quality of the therapist in the client-centred therapy of Carl Rogers (1951). In a famous paper, Rogers (1957) set out the necessary and sufficient conditions for therapeutic personality change. Three of them concern the therapist and are known as the core conditions. Congruence means the therapist is real in the relationship: what they feel inside matches what they say and show, with no professional mask. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance of the client as a person, without conditions of worth. Accurate empathic understanding means sensing the client's private world \"as if\" it were one's own, without losing the \"as if\" quality, and communicating that understanding. Rogers believed that when a client experiences these conditions, the actualising tendency takes over and the client moves toward growth. If a counsellor, Kabir, feels bored but nods with fake enthusiasm, he is incongruent; if he honestly says, \"I notice I am losing the thread; can you help me see what matters most here?\", he is congruent. Option d is right because congruence is one of the three core therapist conditions. Exam trap: incongruity sounds similar but is the client's problem in Rogers's theory, the gap between self-concept and experience that therapy aims to close; and sympathy (feeling sorry for someone) is not empathy (feeling with them).", "topic": "Rogers's core conditions", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "An approach that examines the clients' problems and then uses a variety of techniques that could help the clients are referred to as:", "options": {"a": "Psychiatry", "b": "Eclectic approach", "c": "Resistance approach", "d": "Transference approach"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the eclectic approach. Eclecticism in counselling and psychotherapy means first studying the particular client's problem and then choosing whichever techniques, from whichever school, are most likely to help with it, instead of applying one theory to everyone. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this sequence: examine the problem, then use a variety of techniques. Arnold Lazarus (1967) argued for technical eclecticism and later built multimodal therapy (1976), which assesses seven areas summed up as BASIC I.D. (Behaviour, Affect, Sensation, Imagery, Cognition, Interpersonal relationships, Drugs and biology) and borrows a technique for each problem area, for example relaxation for tension and cognitive restructuring for self-critical thoughts. Technical eclecticism borrows techniques without their theories; psychotherapy integration goes further and fuses theories into a new framework (theoretical integration) or builds on the factors shared by all therapies (common factors). For example, a counsellor seeing Arjun for exam anxiety might teach him breathing and relaxation from behaviour therapy, challenge his 'I must top the class' belief with cognitive techniques, and use the empathic listening of Carl Rogers (1951) to build trust. Exam trap: resistance and transference sound like approaches, but they are psychoanalytic phenomena that occur inside therapy, and psychiatry is a medical profession, not a way of choosing techniques. Also keep eclecticism (picking from many schools) apart from integration (merging schools into one new model).", "topic": "Eclectic approach in counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Nonmaleficence refers to:", "options": {"a": "Above all, do no harm", "b": "Above all, be patient", "c": "Above all, practice self-care", "d": "Above all, be kind"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: nonmaleficence means 'above all, do no harm'. The phrase echoes the Latin maxim primum non nocere, linked to the Hippocratic tradition in medicine, and it asks every helper to avoid actions that could injure a client physically, psychologically or socially, and to weigh the risks of an intervention before using it. Option a is right because nonmaleficence literally means not doing harm (Latin non, not, and maleficus, harmful). Karen Kitchener (1984) set out five moral principles for counselling ethics: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice and fidelity. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979) gave medicine four: respect for autonomy, nonmaleficence, beneficence and justice. The APA Ethics Code (2002) joins the first pair in Principle A, Beneficence and Nonmaleficence. In practice nonmaleficence means not using a technique you are untrained in, avoiding exploitative dual relationships, and not pushing a fragile client into a frightening task without preparation. For example, if Meera's counsellor has never been trained in trauma work, referring her to a trained colleague rather than experimenting on her honours nonmaleficence. Exam trap: nonmaleficence (avoid harm) and beneficence (actively do good) are a look-alike pair that examiners swap. Patience, kindness and self-care are good qualities in a counsellor, but none of them is the definition of a named ethical principle.", "topic": "Nonmaleficence: do no harm", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Providing equality of resources and opportunity for all people upholds the ethical principle of:", "options": {"a": "Veracity", "b": "Autonomy", "c": "Justice", "d": "Fidelity"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: providing equality of resources and opportunity for all people upholds the principle of justice. In professional ethics, justice means fairness: treating people equally unless there is a relevant reason to treat them differently, giving everyone fair access to services, and sharing benefits and burdens evenly regardless of caste, religion, gender, income or disability. Option c is right because 'equality of resources and opportunity' is the textbook wording of justice. It is one of the five principles for counselling set out by Karen Kitchener (1984) (autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, fidelity), one of the four principles of biomedical ethics of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979), and Principle D of the APA Ethics Code (2002), which asks psychologists to give all persons fair access to, and equal quality of, their services. In research, the Belmont Report (1979) applies justice through fair selection of participants, so that one group does not carry all the risks while another enjoys the benefits. For example, a college counselling centre that offers free sessions, a ramp for wheelchair users and counselling in Hindi as well as English, so that Kabir, a labourer's son, can use it as easily as anyone else, is acting on justice. Exam trap: the four ethics words in the options are easily swapped. Veracity is truthfulness, autonomy is the client's right to make their own choices, and fidelity is keeping promises and being trustworthy; only justice is about fairness and equal access.", "topic": "Justice as ethical principle", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The stage of counselling in which client should reach clearer understanding of his or her life concerns and begin to formulate a new sense of hope and direction is called:", "options": {"a": "In-depth exploration", "b": "Commitment to action", "c": "Initial disclosure", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: in-depth exploration. A three-stage model of the counselling process, associated with Lewis Patterson and Elizabeth Welfel's The Counseling Process and repeated in many Indian texts, runs as follows. Stage one, initial disclosure, builds the relationship: the client tells the story, and the counsellor uses attending, listening and primary empathy to create trust. Stage two, in-depth exploration, goes deeper: the counsellor uses more challenging skills such as advanced empathy, immediacy and gentle confrontation, and the client looks at feelings, patterns and blind spots. The goal of this stage is that the client reaches a clearer understanding of his or her life concerns and begins to formulate a new sense of hope and direction. Stage three, commitment to action, decides what the client will actually do: concrete goals, a chosen plan and carrying it out. Option a is right because the stem repeats the goal of the second stage almost word for word: understanding, and only the first stirrings of direction, come before action. The model parallels the three stages of Gerard Egan's The Skilled Helper (first edition 1975), which later editions call the current picture, the preferred picture and the way forward, and the exploration, insight and action stages of Clara Hill and Karen O'Brien (1999). For example, by session four Riya, who came in feeling 'stuck', realises that her distress is about studying for a career her parents chose, and begins to feel she could decide for herself. She has completed in-depth exploration; drawing up steps to talk to her parents is commitment to action. Exam trap: the word 'direction' pulls students toward commitment to action, which is about specific goals, plans and doing. Key note: 'a new sense of direction' could be read as the start of commitment to action, but the model states this outcome as the goal of the second stage, and commitment to action is where that direction becomes goals and steps; a stands.", "topic": "Stages of the counselling process", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Concurrent validity refers to:", "options": {"a": "The two tests are done at the same time", "b": "Two or more clinicians agree on the outcome", "c": "The items on the test consistently relate to each other", "d": "The notion that scores on a test correlate highly with scores from tests that measure the same attribute"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: concurrent validity is shown when scores on a test correlate highly with scores on an established measure of the same attribute taken at about the same time. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. Criterion-related validity checks this against an outside standard, the criterion, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis, a gold-standard scale or job ratings. It has two types that differ only in timing: in concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured together; in predictive validity the criterion comes later, as when entrance scores are correlated with first-year marks. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) discussed concurrent validity as one of the four types of validity named in the APA's 1954 technical recommendations, alongside predictive, content and construct validity. Option d is right because it describes a high correlation with another measure of the same attribute, which is how a new test is checked against an existing one. For example, Kabir writes a 20-item Hindi depression questionnaire, gives it to 100 clinic patients on the same day as the Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961), and finds r = .80: good concurrent validity. Exam trap: option d's wording, 'correlate with tests that measure the same attribute', also describes convergent validity, a part of construct validity; here it is the only option that involves correlating the test with another measure, so it is the answer. Do not let the word 'concurrent' pull you to option a, and do not confuse validity with the reliability ideas in options b and c.", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is assessed by Cronbach alpha?", "options": {"a": "Concurrent validity", "b": "Inter-rater reliability", "c": "Internal consistency", "d": "Test-retest reliability"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: Cronbach's alpha assesses internal consistency. Internal consistency reliability is the degree to which the items of a test hang together, that is, whether they all measure the same underlying construct, judged from a single administration. Lee Cronbach (1951) generalised the KR-20 formula of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937), which suits right-or-wrong items, to items with several score points such as Likert ratings; for items scored 0 or 1, alpha equals KR-20. Alpha is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the arbitrariness of choosing one split. It runs from 0 to 1; about .70 is often taken as acceptable and .80 to .90 as good, while very high values can signal redundant items. Alpha rises with the average inter-item correlation and with the number of items. Option c is right because alpha is computed from the item variances within one sitting, which is exactly what internal consistency means. For example, if Meera's 10-item exam-stress scale gives alpha = .85, her items are measuring one thing consistently. Exam trap: each option pairs with its own statistic. Concurrent validity uses a correlation with a criterion measured at the same time; inter-rater reliability uses Jacob Cohen's (1960) kappa or percentage agreement; test-retest reliability correlates scores from two occasions.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha", "fig": "Lee Cronbach"}, {"q": "The definition of psychological constructs such as love in a way as to allow measurement of it is known as:", "options": {"a": "Conceptualisation", "b": "Scale of measurement", "c": "An operational definition", "d": "Hypothesising"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: an operational definition. An operational definition defines a construct by the operations used to measure or produce it, so that anyone can repeat the measurement. Love, anxiety or intelligence cannot be seen directly, so the researcher states exactly what will count as the variable: love is the score on the Love Scale of Zick Rubin (1970), hunger is hours without food, aggression is the number of times a child hits a Bobo doll in ten minutes. The idea, operationism, came from the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927) in The Logic of Modern Physics, and Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought it into psychology. Option c is right because the stem asks for a definition that allows measurement, which is the whole purpose of an operational definition; it makes research objective and replicable, though no single operation captures every side of a construct. For example, Arjun, studying romantic love among college couples, might operationally define love as the total score on Rubin's 13-item Love Scale, or as the seconds a couple spend looking into each other's eyes during a ten-minute conversation. Exam trap: a conceptual (constitutive) definition explains a construct in words using other concepts ('love is deep affection and attachment'), but it does not tell you how to measure it, so 'conceptualisation' is the near miss examiners offer.", "topic": "Operational definition", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the difference between data measured on an interval scale and on a ratio scale?", "options": {"a": "An interval scale has a true zero point so zero on the scale corresponds to a zero of the concept being measured", "b": "A ratio scale has a true zero point so zero on the scale corresponds to a zero of the concept being measured", "c": "Ratio scale puts data into categories while an interval scale measures on a continuous scale", "d": "A ratio scale has equal intervals between the points on the scale whereas the interval scale does not"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: a ratio scale has a true zero point, so zero on the scale means none of the quantity being measured. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels. Nominal scales name categories (blood group, religion). Ordinal scales rank (position in class). Interval scales have equal distances between points, so differences are meaningful, but the zero is arbitrary: 0 degrees Celsius does not mean no temperature, and an IQ of 0 would not mean no intelligence. Ratio scales have equal intervals and an absolute zero, so ratios are meaningful: a reaction time of 400 milliseconds is truly twice one of 200, and 0 kg means no weight at all. Option b is right because the true zero is the one feature that separates ratio from interval; both have equal intervals. For example, Riya's score of 40 on an anxiety questionnaire does not make her 'twice as anxious' as Kabir with 20, because the questionnaire's zero is not an absence of anxiety, but if she takes 60 seconds to solve a puzzle and he takes 30, she really did take twice as long. Because ratio data have a true zero, every statistic, including the coefficient of variation and the geometric mean, can be used on them. Exam trap: option a is the same sentence with interval and ratio swapped. Remember the order NOIR (nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio): each level adds one property, and the R for ratio adds the real zero.", "topic": "Interval versus ratio scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Rating scales are used to record judgements about:", "options": {"a": "Oneself", "b": "Objects", "c": "Others", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: rating scales are used to record judgements about oneself, about other people and about objects. A rating scale asks a judge to place something on a set of ordered points, such as 1 to 5 or 'never' to 'always', so that an impression becomes a number. Formats include numerical, graphic (a line to mark), descriptive and behaviourally anchored scales, the summated agreement scale of Rensis Likert (1932) and the semantic differential of Charles Osgood (1957). Option d is right because each listed target is a standard use. About oneself: self-rating scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961), on which Meera rates her own sadness, sleep and appetite. About others: a teacher rates a pupil's attention, a supervisor rates an employee, a clinician rates a patient on the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (Max Hamilton, 1960), or a parent rates a child's behaviour. About objects: consumers rate products, judges rate paintings, and students rate an idea or a picture on a semantic differential (good to bad, strong to weak). Because human judges are involved, rating scales carry rater errors: the halo effect named by Edward Thorndike (1920), leniency and severity, and central tendency (avoiding the extremes). Exam trap: each single option is true, which is why examiners offer them; picking one alone ignores the other two uses. When every listed target is a genuine use, choose 'all of the above'.", "topic": "Uses of rating scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Projective techniques have been popular with clinical psychologists, their validity is considered _____ among tests of personality.", "options": {"a": "Highest", "b": "Moderate", "c": "Lowest", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: although projective techniques have long been popular with clinical psychologists, their validity is generally considered the lowest among tests of personality. Projective tests, such as the inkblots of Hermann Rorschach (1921), the Thematic Apperception Test of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), sentence completion and figure drawings, present an ambiguous stimulus and assume that people project their needs and conflicts into their answers. That makes faking harder and yields rich clinical material. The trouble is psychometric. Many techniques have several competing scoring systems, interpretation is often global and intuitive, norms are weak, retest reliability is poor because stories and percepts change, and even scored indices add little beyond an interview and an inventory (poor incremental validity). Scott Lilienfeld, James Wood and Howard Garb (2000), reviewing the evidence, found support for only a few Rorschach indices, such as those for thought disorder, and little for most others or for figure drawings. Loren Chapman and Jean Chapman (1967, 1969) showed why clinicians keep trusting invalid signs: illusory correlation. Option c is right because, compared with objective inventories such as the MMPI, which have the strongest validity data, projectives sit at the bottom. For example, if one clinician reads Kabir's TAT stories as 'aggressive', a second clinician using another system may disagree, and the label may not predict how he actually behaves. Exam trap: popularity is not validity; the stem's 'have been popular' sets up a contrast, not a clue that validity is high. The Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) improved Rorschach scoring reliability, but it did not lift projectives to the validity of inventories.", "topic": "Validity of projective techniques", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In administration of Rorschach, the inquiry refers to that part of the examination in which:", "options": {"a": "Complete demographic information is obtained", "b": "Cards are exposed to the test taker for the first time", "c": "Cards are exposed to the test taker for the second time", "d": "The test taker is permitted to ask the examiner any question at all"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach, the inquiry is the part of the examination in which the cards are shown to the test taker for the second time. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published ten inkblots: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) administration has two main phases. In the response phase (free association) the examiner hands over the cards one at a time in a fixed order, asks 'What might this be?', and records every response word for word without suggesting answers. In the inquiry phase, after all ten cards, the examiner goes back through each card and each response to learn where on the blot it was seen (location), what made it look that way, such as form, colour, shading or movement (determinants), and what it was (content), using non-leading questions such as 'What made it look like that?'. An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow, in which the examiner asks directly about popular responses or determinants the person never used. Option c is right because the inquiry depends on re-exposing the same cards so that each response can be scored. For example, if Riya said 'a bat' to Card I, in the inquiry the examiner hands her Card I again and asks her to show where the bat is and what made it look like one. Exam trap: option b, the first exposure, is the response phase; the inquiry always comes after it and never uses leading questions.", "topic": "Rorschach inquiry phase", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "Psychometric soundness of projective technique like RIB is difficult to evaluate because:", "options": {"a": "Traditional construct validity evaluation procedures are not applicable", "b": "Traditional test-retest reliability procedures are not applicable", "c": "It taps enduring traits, not enduring states", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the psychometric soundness of a projective technique such as the Rorschach Inkblot test is difficult to evaluate because traditional test-retest reliability procedures are not applicable. Reliability is normally checked in standard ways: test-retest gives the same test twice and correlates the scores, and split-half divides the items into two equivalent halves. The point comes from the assessment text of Ronald Cohen and Mark Swerdlik, and the reasons are easy to see. Responses are open and free, so they can change from one occasion to the next with the person's mood, recent experiences and fatigue (passing states, not only stable traits), and on a retest people may remember and repeat, or deliberately vary, their earlier answers. A change in responses therefore does not show unreliability in the usual sense. Split-half is also unsuitable, because the ten blots are not interchangeable items: each card has its own shape and colour and pulls different responses, so no two halves are equivalent. Option b is right because it names this reliability problem directly. For example, if Arjun sees threatening animals in the blots the day after a frightening exam result and gentler figures a month later, the change may reflect his state, not measurement error. Exam trap: option c reverses the problem; a test tapping enduring traits would suit test-retest, and the difficulty is that responses also reflect shifting states. Key note: in the study quiz for Cohen and Swerdlik's Psychological Testing and Assessment, option c reads 'taps enduring traits, not transient states' and is still not a reason for the difficulty, so d cannot be right; one could argue a as well, since construct validation of projectives is hard, but that quiz gives b, so b stands.", "topic": "Reliability problems of projectives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "To prevent ethical abuse in psychological research the APA has suggested that:", "options": {"a": "Psychologists must treat all subjects with respect and concern for the subject's dignity", "b": "Psychologists must avoid deception while using human subjects", "c": "All data collected from the persons must be made public", "d": "All psychological harm to the subject must be corrected with counselling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: to prevent ethical abuse in research, the APA asks psychologists to treat all participants with respect and concern for their dignity. The American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016) has five aspirational General Principles. Principle E, Respect for People's Rights and Dignity, covers privacy, confidentiality and self-determination, and the enforceable standards (mainly Standard 8, with Standard 4 on privacy and confidentiality) turn it into research rules: informed consent before the study, freedom to withdraw, confidentiality of data, minimising harm and debriefing afterwards. Option a is right because respect for participants' dignity is the umbrella principle from which these rules follow, and it holds without exceptions. The other options are distorted versions of real rules. Deception is not banned: Standard 8.07 allows it when the study has significant scientific value, no effective non-deceptive method is feasible, nobody is deceived about anything expected to cause physical pain or severe emotional distress, and the deception is explained in debriefing. Data are kept confidential, not made public. Harm must be minimised and, if it occurs, reasonable steps taken to reduce it, but there is no rule that every harm be corrected by counselling. For example, when Kabir takes part in a conformity study with a cover story, he is told the real aim at the end, may withdraw his data, and his answers are stored under a code number. Exam trap: absolute words ('must avoid', 'all', 'must be made public') mark the distorted options; the real APA rules on deception and harm are conditional.", "topic": "APA research ethics principles", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Three major concerns for psychological researchers are:", "options": {"a": "Deception, lasting harm to subjects", "b": "Loss of future research possibilities", "c": "Falsified results", "d": "Invasion of privacy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: deception and lasting harm to subjects are two of the three major ethical concerns for psychological researchers; the third is invasion of privacy. Introductory psychology texts commonly sum up research ethics in these three worries. Deception means misleading participants about the purpose or procedures of a study (a cover story, a confederate, false feedback); it is allowed only when justified and followed by debriefing. Lasting harm means psychological, physical or social damage that outlives the study, such as lowered self-esteem, guilt or stress; participants should leave no worse off than they came. Invasion of privacy means collecting or revealing personal information without consent, or observing people where they expect privacy; confidentiality and anonymity guard against it. The obedience study of Stanley Milgram (1963) is the classic case: participants were deceived about the shocks and many showed intense stress, raising questions of deception and harm together. Option a is right because it is the option that holds two of the three named concerns. For example, if Riya secretly records classmates' phone chats for a study on gossip, she invades their privacy; if she also falsely tells them they failed an IQ test to watch their reactions, she uses deception that may cause lasting harm unless she debriefs them. Exam trap: falsified results is serious research misconduct, but it is the researcher's dishonesty, not a concern about how participants are treated. Key note: as printed, the item looks like a three-part list (deception, lasting harm, invasion of privacy) split across options a and d; a holds two of the three named concerns, so a stands.", "topic": "Major ethical concerns in research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If groups of participants are selected to represent sub-groups in the population (e.g. such as selecting entire class of psychology students to be compared to a group of history students). This is known as:", "options": {"a": "Cluster sampling", "b": "Simple random sampling", "c": "Haphazard sampling", "d": "Opportunity sampling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: cluster sampling. In cluster sampling the researcher selects whole, naturally occurring groups (clusters) such as classes, schools, villages or hospital wards, usually at random, and studies everyone, or a random sample, inside each chosen group. Only a list of clusters is needed, not a list of every individual, which makes it practical for large or scattered populations; when the clusters are geographical areas it is also called area sampling. Option a is right because the stem's cue is 'selecting an entire class': the unit chosen is an intact group, not individual students. The cost is precision. Ideally each cluster is a mixed miniature of the population, but in practice people in one class or village tend to be alike, so sampling error is larger than for a simple random sample of the same size. Compare stratified sampling, where the population is first divided into strata (such as gender or year of study) and individuals are drawn at random from every stratum, so that each subgroup is represented. For example, to compare the attitudes of psychology and history students, Meera takes all 40 students of one psychology section and all 40 of one history section, rather than picking individuals from the whole university list. Exam trap: clusters and strata are easily swapped. In stratified sampling you sample individuals within every stratum; in cluster sampling you sample whole groups and take them intact. Key note: the phrase 'to represent sub-groups' sounds like stratified sampling, but stratified sampling is not offered, and taking an entire intact class is the defining move of cluster sampling, so a stands.", "topic": "Cluster sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a benefit of using simple random sampling?", "options": {"a": "Accuracy of results can be calculated", "b": "Results are always representative", "c": "Interviewers can choose respondents freely", "d": "Informants can refuse to participate"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: a benefit of simple random sampling is that the accuracy of the results can be calculated. In simple random sampling every member of the population has an equal and independent chance of being chosen, using the lottery (fishbowl) method, a table of random numbers or a computer. Because selection follows known probability rules, statistical theory tells us how far a sample figure is likely to differ from the true population value: the standard error can be computed, and from it a margin of error and a confidence interval. Option a is right because this ability to estimate sampling error is the main advantage of probability sampling over non-probability methods such as quota or convenience sampling, where the size of the error cannot be known. Random selection also removes the researcher's personal bias in choosing people. For example, if Arjun randomly selects 400 of a college's 4,000 students and finds that 30 percent report poor sleep, he can state that the true figure very probably lies within about 4 to 5 percentage points of 30 percent (a 95 percent confidence interval). Exam trap: random sampling makes a representative sample likely, not certain; any single random sample can, by bad luck, miss a small subgroup, so 'always representative' is the overstatement examiners plant.", "topic": "Simple random sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In the equation of straight line Y = mX + c, if m is equal to zero then:", "options": {"a": "X increases Y decreases", "b": "X increases Y remains constant", "c": "Y increases X decreases", "d": "X increases Y increases"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: if m = 0 in Y = mX + c, then as X increases, Y remains constant. In the equation of a straight line, m is the slope (gradient): the change in Y for every one-unit increase in X. c is the Y-intercept, the value of Y where the line crosses the Y axis, that is, when X = 0. If m is positive the line rises (as X increases, Y increases); if m is negative it falls (as X increases, Y decreases); if m = 0 the equation becomes Y = 0 x X + c, so Y = c for every value of X and the line is horizontal. Option b is right because a zero slope means a change in X brings no change in Y. In statistics the same line is written Y = a + bX in simple linear regression, the method that grew out of the work of Francis Galton (1886) on regression to the mean, where b is the regression coefficient (the slope). A zero slope there means the predictor tells you nothing about the criterion, and the correlation between them is zero. For example, if Kabir's exam marks stay at 60 however many hours (X) he spends listening to music, the line is Y = 60: m = 0 and c = 60. Exam trap: option c describes a negative relation stated from Y's side, which needs m less than zero, not m = 0. Also do not mix up m and c: c = 0 would only make the line pass through the origin.", "topic": "Slope of a straight line", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the other name of Chi square goodness of fit test?", "options": {"a": "Chi square ANOVA", "b": "Two sample Chi square", "c": "Wilcoxon", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: none of these, because the chi-square goodness-of-fit test is also called the one-sample (one-way, or one-variable) chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test for frequency data, where people are counted into categories rather than given scores. It compares the observed frequency (O) in each category with the frequency expected (E) under the null hypothesis: chi-square = sum of (O - E) squared / E. The goodness-of-fit test uses one categorical variable and asks whether its frequencies match a hypothesised pattern, such as equal preference or known population proportions; its degrees of freedom are k - 1 for k categories. The chi-square test of independence uses two variables in a contingency table and asks whether they are associated; its degrees of freedom are (rows - 1) x (columns - 1). Option d is right because none of the three listed names refers to the goodness-of-fit test. For example, Riya asks 120 students which of four chai brands they prefer; equal preference predicts 30 each. She observes 45, 30, 25 and 20, so chi-square = (225 + 0 + 25 + 100) / 30 = 11.67 with 3 degrees of freedom. That exceeds the .05 critical value of 7.81, so the preferences are not equal. Exam trap: 'two-sample chi-square' sounds like the partner of a one-sample test, but the chi-square that involves two variables is the test of independence (or homogeneity), not goodness of fit.", "topic": "Chi-square goodness of fit", "fig": ""}, {"q": "As the sample size increases the shape of the t-distribution changes to:", "options": {"a": "Broader", "b": "Skewed", "c": "Flatter", "d": "Normal looking"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: as sample size increases, the t distribution becomes more and more normal looking. William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, published the distribution under the pen name 'Student', hence Student's t. It is used when the population standard deviation is unknown and must be estimated from the sample, which adds extra uncertainty. The t distribution is a family of curves, one for each number of degrees of freedom (df = n - 1 for one sample). Like the normal curve, each is symmetrical and bell-shaped around zero; unlike it, a t curve has a lower peak and heavier tails, because small samples give unsteady estimates of the standard deviation. As df rise the estimate steadies, the tails thin and the curve closes in on the standard normal (z) curve; by about 30 df the two are very close, and with infinite df they are identical. That is why the two-tailed .05 critical value falls from 12.71 at df = 1 to 2.04 at df = 30, and towards 1.96 beyond. Option d is right because larger samples make t approach the normal shape. For example, with 5 students Meera needs a t of about 2.78 for significance at .05, while with 101 students about 1.98 is enough. Exam trap: 'broader' and 'flatter' describe the small-sample t, the reverse of what happens as n grows, and t is never skewed at any sample size.", "topic": "Shape of the t distribution", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Another expression of constant variance is:", "options": {"a": "Leptokurtosis", "b": "Homoscedasticity", "c": "Heteroscedasticity", "d": "Covariance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: homoscedasticity is another expression for constant variance. The word comes from the Greek homo (same) and skedasis (scattering). In regression and correlation it means that the scatter of Y, or of the residuals (prediction errors), is about the same at every value of X; in group comparisons such as the t test and ANOVA the same idea is called homogeneity of variance, meaning equal variances across groups. It is a key assumption of these parametric methods and is checked with a residual plot or with tests such as those of Howard Levene (1960) and Maurice Bartlett (1937) or the F-max test of H. O. Hartley (1950). Its violation, heteroscedasticity, shows as a fan or funnel shape in which the errors widen as X grows, and it makes standard errors and significance tests untrustworthy. Option b is right because 'constant variance' is the literal meaning of homoscedasticity. For example, if Kabir predicts monthly spending from income and finds that spending varies little among low earners but hugely among high earners, his residual plot fans out: that is heteroscedasticity, not constant variance. Exam trap: homoscedasticity and heteroscedasticity are opposites that examiners swap; homo means same, hetero means different. Leptokurtosis describes the peak and tails of a single distribution, and covariance describes how two variables move together; neither is about equal spread.", "topic": "Homoscedasticity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An orientation towards understanding mental processes such as perceiving, remembering, reasoning, deciding and problem solving and their relationship to behaviour is called:", "options": {"a": "Behavioural", "b": "Cognitive", "c": "Biological", "d": "Subjectivistic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: an orientation towards understanding mental processes such as perceiving, remembering, reasoning, deciding and problem solving, and how they relate to behaviour, is the cognitive perspective. A perspective is a broad viewpoint that decides which questions psychologists ask. The cognitive perspective treats the mind as an active processor of information: input is attended to, encoded, stored, retrieved and used to make decisions. It grew out of the cognitive revolution of the 1950s, when George Miller (1956) showed that short-term memory holds about seven items and Noam Chomsky (1959) argued that language cannot be explained by reinforcement alone. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the field in his book Cognitive Psychology. Because mental processes cannot be seen directly, researchers infer them from measurable data such as reaction times, recall scores and errors. Example: when Meera forgets a phone number because a friend interrupts her, a cognitive psychologist asks how much information working memory holds and why interference wipes it out. Option b is right because the stem lists exactly the processes that define this perspective and adds their link to behaviour. Exam trap: the behavioural perspective also studies behaviour, so examiners count on the shared word; but John B. Watson (1913) excluded mental processes from science as unobservable, and B. F. Skinner (1938) rejected them as causes of behaviour, so a viewpoint built around perceiving and reasoning cannot be behavioural.", "topic": "Cognitive perspective", "fig": "Ulric Neisser"}, {"q": "Condition in which factors other than independent variables are permitted to vary across experimental conditions, invalidating the results is called:", "options": {"a": "Confounding", "b": "Experimenter effect", "c": "Placebo effect", "d": "Deception"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: when factors other than the independent variable are allowed to vary across experimental conditions, so that the results cannot be trusted, the experiment is confounded. In an experiment the researcher changes one thing, the independent variable (IV), and measures its effect on the dependent variable (DV). Every other factor should be held constant or spread evenly across groups by random assignment. A confounding variable is an extraneous variable that changes systematically along with the IV, so any difference in the DV could be due to either. The study then loses internal validity: you can no longer claim that the IV caused the effect. Example: Kabir tests whether music improves memory, but the music group learns in the morning and the silence group in the evening. Time of day is confounded with music, so a better score in the music group proves nothing. Controls include random assignment, matching, counterbalancing and keeping conditions identical. Option a is right because 'other factors permitted to vary across conditions, invalidating the results' is the textbook definition of confounding. Exam trap: the experimenter effect and the placebo effect are themselves specific sources of confounding, so they sound right; but the stem gives the general definition, and the general term is confounding.", "topic": "Confounding variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "People's report of how they perceive an object to be and how bright a flash of light seems to be is an example of:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Introspection", "c": "Cathartic effect", "d": "Law of effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: asking people to report how an object looks to them, or how bright a flash of light seems, is introspection. Introspection means 'looking inward': a trained observer examines his or her own conscious experience of a stimulus and describes it. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) used it in the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig, but not as casual self-reflection. His observers saw controlled stimuli such as lights, tones and metronome beats and gave short, immediate judgements of their sensations and feelings. Edward Titchener, working at Cornell, developed a stricter version that he named structural psychology (1898), training observers to report only basic elements (sensations, images and feelings) and not to name the object itself, which he called the 'stimulus error'. Example: Riya watches two flashes in a laboratory and reports that the second seems brighter and slightly yellow; her report of her own experience is introspective data. Introspection lost ground because observers in different laboratories disagreed and private reports could not be checked; John B. Watson (1913) rejected it entirely. Option b is right because the stem describes people reporting their own perceptual experience. Exam trap: reports of how bright a light seems are also the raw material of psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner (1860), but psychophysics is not offered here; among the four options, only introspection is a method of self-report.", "topic": "Introspection", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "Part of neuron that conducts action potential away from the cell body is:", "options": {"a": "Synapse", "b": "Dendrites", "c": "Axon", "d": "Glial cells"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the axon is the part of a neuron that conducts the action potential away from the cell body. A typical neuron has three main parts. Dendrites are branching fibres that receive messages from other cells. The cell body (soma) contains the nucleus and adds up the incoming signals. The axon is a single long fibre that leaves the soma at the axon hillock. When the summed input reaches threshold, an action potential (a brief, all-or-none electrical impulse) starts near the hillock and travels down the axon to the axon terminals, which release neurotransmitter onto the next cell. Many axons are wrapped in a fatty myelin sheath broken by gaps called nodes of Ranvier, so the impulse jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction) and travels much faster. A simple memory aid: dendrites deliver signals to the cell, the axon carries them away. Option c is right because the axon is the only listed structure that carries the impulse away from the soma. Exam trap: dendrites carry graded signals the other way, towards the cell body, and examiners often swap 'towards' and 'away from' to catch students who learned the parts without the direction of flow.", "topic": "Neuron structure: the axon", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A form of conditioning in which the onset of the Unconditioned Stimulus (UCS) begins while the conditioned stimulus is still present is called:", "options": {"a": "Trace conditioning", "b": "Delay conditioning", "c": "Simultaneous conditioning", "d": "Backward conditioning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: when the conditioned stimulus (CS) starts first and is still present when the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) begins, the arrangement is delay conditioning. In Ivan Pavlov's (1927) classical conditioning, learning depends heavily on the timing of the CS (for example, a tone) and the UCS (for example, food). In delay (delayed) conditioning, one of the forward arrangements, the CS comes on, stays on, and overlaps with the UCS. The 'delay' is the interval before the UCS arrives, not slowness of learning. It is usually the most effective arrangement, because the CS works as a warning signal that predicts the UCS. Example: in a laboratory, a tone sounds and keeps sounding until a puff of air hits Arjun's eye; after a number of pairings he blinks as soon as the tone comes on, before any puff arrives. Option b is right because the stem's key detail, that the UCS begins while the CS is still present, defines the overlap of delay conditioning. Exam trap: trace conditioning is also a forward arrangement (CS first), so students confuse the two; the difference is that in trace conditioning the CS ends before the UCS starts, leaving a gap that must be bridged by a memory 'trace', and learning is weaker.", "topic": "Delay conditioning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The learning in which material to be learned is broken up into small easy steps is called:", "options": {"a": "Operant conditioning", "b": "Programmed learning", "c": "Social learning", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: learning in which the material is broken up into small, easy steps is programmed learning (programmed instruction). B. F. Skinner (1954) argued in 'The Science of Learning and the Art of Teaching' that classrooms reinforce correct answers too rarely and too late. He applied operant principles to teaching: content is divided into small units called frames; the learner makes an active response to each frame; feedback, which acts as reinforcement, is immediate; and each learner moves at his or her own pace, with steps small enough that errors are rare. He delivered these programmes on teaching machines, building on the machine Sidney Pressey built in the 1920s. In a linear programme (Skinner) every learner follows the same sequence of frames; in a branching programme, developed by Norman Crowder in the late 1950s, a wrong answer sends the learner to a remedial frame. Example: Meera learns the parts of the eye from an app that shows one fact, asks one question, tells her at once whether she is right, and only then moves on. Option b is right because breaking material into small, easy steps is the defining feature of programmed learning. Exam trap: programmed learning is built on operant conditioning, so option a tempts; but operant conditioning is the general learning process (behaviour shaped by its consequences), while programmed learning is the teaching method that applies it.", "topic": "Programmed learning", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Multi-factor theory of intelligence by Thorndike is at one extreme of the interpretation regarding the nature of:", "options": {"a": "Intellectual organisation", "b": "Reasoning", "c": "Mental organisation", "d": "Motor organisation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike's (1927) multifactor theory sits at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of mental organisation, that is, how the abilities that make up intelligence are structured. Theorists who correlated many tests asked whether the abilities behind them form one general power, a few broad group factors or a host of separate bits. Thorndike gave the most splintered answer. For him intelligence is made up of a multitude of separate, minute elements of ability, each a set of stimulus-response connections. Any mental act uses a number of these elements together, two tasks correlate only to the extent that they share elements, and there is no general intelligence: a brighter person simply has more connections. The theory is therefore called atomistic. Even so, Thorndike grouped tasks into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence. At the other extreme stands Charles Spearman's (1904) two-factor theory, in which all intellectual activity depends mainly on one general factor, g, plus a specific factor, s, for each task; group-factor theories such as that of Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) sit in between. Example: Kabir repairs engines brilliantly but is average at abstract puzzles; Thorndike would call these largely separate abilities. Option c is right because the range from multifactor to two-factor theories is a range of views on how the mind's abilities are organised, which is exactly the phrase the textbook uses. Exam trap: the opposite pole is Spearman's g, not Thurstone's primary mental abilities, which are a middle position; and do not mix up Thorndike (multifactor) with Thurstone (primary mental abilities). Key note: the stem copies a sentence from Frank S. Freeman's Theory and Practice of Psychological Testing (third edition, 1962), which reads 'the nature of mental organization', so c is the answer the paper intends. Option a, intellectual organisation, means much the same thing here; if you picked it you understood the idea, but on this paper c is the safe mark, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Multifactor theory of intelligence", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "Who developed the concept of primary mental abilities?", "options": {"a": "R.B. Cattell", "b": "E.L. Thorndike", "c": "L.L. Thurstone", "d": "J.P. Guilford"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) developed the concept of primary mental abilities. Charles Spearman (1904) had argued that a single general factor, g, runs through all intellectual tasks. Thurstone gave a large battery of tests to college students and analysed the scores with his own method, multiple factor analysis. Instead of one g he found seven relatively independent group factors, which he called primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning (induction). He argued that a person is better described by a profile across these abilities than by a single IQ, and built tests to measure each one. Later analyses showed that the seven abilities correlate with one another, so a general factor reappeared at a higher level, a point Thurstone himself later accepted. Example: Arjun scores high on space and number but low on word fluency; Thurstone would report that uneven profile instead of averaging it into one score. Option c is right because 'primary mental abilities' is Thurstone's own term for his seven factors. Exam trap: all four names are factor theorists of intelligence, so the item tests matching each theorist to his model: g and s for Spearman, primary mental abilities for Thurstone, fluid and crystallised intelligence for Raymond B. Cattell (1963), structure of intellect for J. P. Guilford (1967), and abstract, mechanical and social intelligence for Edward Thorndike (1927).", "topic": "Primary mental abilities", "fig": "Louis Leon Thurstone"}, {"q": "The life goal of an individual is a/an:", "options": {"a": "Biological motivation", "b": "Instinct", "c": "Social motivation", "d": "Personal motivation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: an individual's life goal is classed as a personal motive. Indian textbooks commonly sort motives into three groups. Biological (physiological or primary) motives are unlearned and rooted in bodily needs: hunger, thirst, sleep, sex and pain avoidance. Social (psychosocial) motives are learned through living in groups and are satisfied through other people: achievement, affiliation, power and approval. Personal motives belong to one individual and grow out of his or her own history: habits, interests, attitudes, level of aspiration and life goals. A life goal is personal because it is chosen and pursued by one person and differs widely between people, even within the same family or culture. Example: Riya and her brother grow up in the same home, but Riya's life goal is to become a clinical psychologist while her brother wants to run the family shop; each goal belongs to its owner. Option d is right because the standard list places life goals, along with level of aspiration and interests, among personal motives. Exam trap: a life goal sounds social because family and society shape it, and achievement is a social motive; but the classification asks whose motive it is, and a particular goal adopted by one person is personal. Key note: some books fold life goals and level of aspiration into social or psychosocial motives, since they are learned in a social setting, which makes c look possible. The more common three-way scheme (biological, social, personal) lists life goals under personal motives, so d stands.", "topic": "Classification of motives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Self-actualizing tendency according to Maslow is a/an:", "options": {"a": "Instinct", "b": "Deficiency motivation", "c": "Imprinting", "d": "Growth motivation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: for Abraham Maslow (1954), the self-actualising tendency is growth motivation. Maslow arranged human needs in a hierarchy: physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem and, at the top, self-actualisation, becoming everything one is capable of becoming. He split the motives behind these needs into two kinds. The four lower needs are deficiency needs (D-needs): a lack creates tension, the person acts to fill the gap, and once it is filled the need stops motivating, like hunger after a meal. Self-actualisation works differently. It is a growth or being need (B-need) that does not arise from a lack; satisfying it makes it stronger, not weaker, and it is never finished. In Toward a Psychology of Being (1962) Maslow contrasted this growth motivation with deficiency motivation; he later called the motivation of self-actualisers metamotivation, directed at being values such as truth, beauty and justice. Example: Kabir has a secure job, close friends and respect at work, yet he takes up painting simply to develop himself; nothing is missing, he is growing. Option d is right because self-actualisation is Maslow's model case of growth rather than deficiency motivation. Exam trap: Maslow did call human needs 'instinctoid', meaning weak, instinct-like tendencies that culture can easily override, which makes 'instinct' tempting; but instinctoid is not instinct, since instincts are fixed and unlearned, and the textbook label for self-actualisation is growth motivation.", "topic": "Maslow: growth motivation", "fig": "Abraham Maslow"}, {"q": "According to James-Lange theory, what among the following informs the brain that it is experiencing emotions?", "options": {"a": "Hypothalamus", "b": "Amygdala", "c": "Physiological feedback from the body", "d": "Visual sensation of emotional stimuli"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to the James-Lange theory, the brain learns that it is experiencing an emotion from physiological feedback from the body. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) proposed the idea independently. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble. James reversed the order: the stimulus triggers bodily changes first (racing heart, trembling, fast breathing, tense muscles), and the emotion is our perception of those changes. In his words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Without the bodily response, he argued, the perception would stay a pale, purely intellectual judgement with no emotional warmth. Each emotion would therefore need its own pattern of bodily response. Example: Arjun's motorbike skids on a wet road; his heart pounds and his hands shake before he has time to think, and only as he notices this pounding does he feel fear. Later facial feedback research, in which holding a smile slightly lifts mood, gives the theory partial support. Option c is right because, for James and Lange, the feeling is built from signals fed back to the brain from the viscera and muscles. Exam trap: option d describes the first step, seeing the stimulus, which every theory shares; the James-Lange point is that seeing alone does not create the emotion, while the body's response, fed back to the brain, does.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}, {"q": "When Walter Cannon removed the sympathetic nervous system of the cat what did he find?", "options": {"a": "Cat fell into the coma and died", "b": "Cat still showed physiological arousal", "c": "Cat still showed emotions (anger, fear and pleasure)", "d": "Cat showed predatory aggression but not affective attack"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: when Walter Cannon removed the sympathetic nervous system of cats, the cats still showed emotional behaviour such as anger, fear and pleasure. The James-Lange theory says that an emotion is the perception of bodily (visceral) changes, so cutting the viscera off from the brain should abolish it. Walter Cannon, with J. T. Lewis and S. W. Britton (1927), kept cats healthy for many months after removing the entire sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system, the division that acts in great excitement. These cats could no longer make sympathetic blood-vessel responses, release adrenaline from the adrenal medulla, mobilise sugar from the liver or erect their hair. Yet faced with a barking dog they showed all the superficial signs of rage: hissing, growling, ears drawn back, teeth bared and a paw lifted to strike; only the bristling fur was missing. In his 1927 critique of James-Lange, Cannon set these cats beside Charles Sherrington's (1900) dogs, whose viscera had been cut off from the brain, and concluded that the operated animals behaved 'as angrily, as joyfully, as fearfully as ever'. This was the first of his five criticisms; the others were that the same visceral changes occur in different emotions and in non-emotional states, the viscera are too insensitive, visceral changes are too slow, and injected adrenaline produces arousal without true emotion. Cannon proposed instead a thalamic theory, extended by Philip Bard (1928) and now called the Cannon-Bard theory: the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same time. Example: on this view, if Meera's heart could not race, she would still feel angry when insulted. Option c is right because emotional behaviour survived without sympathetic feedback, which the James-Lange theory could not predict. Exam trap: option b reverses the finding; sympathetic arousal is exactly what the surgery removed, so the striking result was emotion without arousal.", "topic": "Cannon's critique of James-Lange", "fig": "Walter Cannon"}, {"q": "Timbre is the quality of sound resulting from:", "options": {"a": "Complexity of sound wave", "b": "Frequency of a sound wave", "c": "Loudness", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: timbre is the quality of a sound that comes from the complexity of the sound wave. Hearing turns three physical properties of a sound wave into three experiences. Frequency, the number of cycles per second measured in hertz (Hz), is heard as pitch; humans hear about 20 to 20,000 Hz. Amplitude, the height of the wave, is heard as loudness, measured in decibels. Complexity, the mixture of frequencies in the wave, is heard as timbre, or tone quality. Almost no natural sound is a pure tone. A musical note has a fundamental frequency, which sets its pitch, plus overtones (harmonics) at multiples of that frequency. Each instrument or voice produces its own blend of overtones, which gives its wave a distinctive shape. Example: Meera plays a note on a sitar and Kabir plays the same note on a flute at the same loudness; pitch and loudness match, yet anyone can tell the two instruments apart, because the overtone mix, the timbre, differs. Option a is right because timbre depends on the complexity of the waveform. Exam trap: frequency (option b) is the commonest wrong pick, since pitch and timbre both sound like 'quality' of a note; frequency gives pitch only, and loudness comes from amplitude.", "topic": "Timbre and sound waves", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which among the following is an underlying mechanism of perceptual constancy?", "options": {"a": "Bottom up processing", "b": "Misleading perception that distorts or misjudges the stimuli", "c": "Top-down processing", "d": "The organisation of perception by beginning with the low lying features"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: perceptual constancy rests on top-down processing. Perceptual constancy is the tendency to see objects as keeping the same size, shape, brightness and colour even though the image on the retina keeps changing. A friend walking away casts a smaller and smaller retinal image, yet she does not seem to shrink. A door swinging open projects a trapezoid, yet we see a rectangle. The brain achieves this by bringing knowledge and context to the raw input: the object's familiar size, how far away it seems, the angle of view and the surrounding light. Hermann von Helmholtz (1867) called this unconscious inference. Using prior knowledge and expectation to interpret sensory data is top-down (conceptually driven) processing. Example: Riya sees her father at the far end of a railway platform; his retinal image is tiny, but because she knows his height and judges the distance, he looks his normal size. Option c is right because constancy depends on knowledge, expectation and context, not only on the features striking the retina. Exam trap: option d is a disguised definition of bottom-up processing (building perception from low-level features), placed beside option a so that bottom-up seems to be offered twice. Key note: some constancy cues, such as the ratio of an object's brightness to its surroundings, are computed early and partly bottom-up, so the mechanism is not purely top-down; but the standard textbook explanation stresses knowledge and inference, so c stands.", "topic": "Perceptual constancy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Among the following which would present the greatest loss to the professional chef?", "options": {"a": "Vestibular loss", "b": "Olfactory loss", "c": "Kinaesthetic loss", "d": "Injury to the cochlea"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: an olfactory loss (loss of the sense of smell) would hurt a professional chef the most. What we call the taste of food is really flavour, and flavour is taste plus smell. The taste buds on the tongue detect only five basic tastes: sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami. Everything else, such as the difference between cardamom and cinnamon, fresh and stale ghee, or mango and banana, comes from odour molecules that travel from the mouth up the back of the nose to the olfactory epithelium (retronasal smell). People who lose smell (anosmia), after a head injury or a viral infection such as COVID-19, often say food has 'lost its taste', even though their taste buds still work. A chef makes fine flavour judgements all day, so losing smell would cripple the job. Example: Arjun, a chef, has a heavy cold; he can tell that a curry is salty, but he cannot tell whether he added too much garam masala. Option b is right because smell supplies most of the flavour a chef must judge. Exam trap: students look for a taste option, and when none is offered they may pick a sense that sounds more serious, such as hearing; the sense that matters most for flavour is smell.", "topic": "Smell and flavour", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Inductive reasoning goes from specific to the general. Which among the following is analogous to inductive reasoning?", "options": {"a": "Top-down processing", "b": "Bottom-up processing", "c": "Perceptual constancies", "d": "Illusions"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: bottom-up processing is analogous to inductive reasoning. Inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to a general conclusion: Kabir sees that every crow he has met is black and concludes that crows are black. Deductive reasoning moves the other way, from a general rule to a specific case. Perception can run in the same two directions. In bottom-up (data-driven) processing, the brain starts with small sensory features, such as lines, edges, angles and colours picked up by feature detector cells, and builds them up into a whole object. The direct perception theory of James J. Gibson (1966) is the classic bottom-up account. In top-down (conceptually driven) processing, knowledge, expectations and context guide how the features are read, as when you make out a badly written word correctly because of the sentence around it. Option b is right because both inductive reasoning and bottom-up processing build the general (a conclusion, a whole percept) from particulars (single observations, single features). Exam trap: top-down processing is the parallel of deductive reasoning, from general knowledge down to a specific interpretation; reversing the pairing is the trap, so remember that bottom-up and inductive both climb from parts to whole.", "topic": "Bottom-up and top-down processing", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which measure is directly related to the variance and is figured by taking the square root of variance?", "options": {"a": "Average deviation", "b": "Quartile deviation", "c": "Standard deviation", "d": "Range"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the standard deviation (SD) is the measure obtained by taking the square root of the variance. Both describe how spread out scores are around the mean. To find the variance, take each score's deviation from the mean, square it so that negative and positive deviations do not cancel, and average the squares: for a population, variance = Σ(X minus M)² ÷ N. Squaring leaves the result in squared units (marks squared), which is hard to interpret. Taking the square root returns it to the original units, giving the SD, the typical distance of a score from the mean. Karl Pearson (1894) introduced the name 'standard deviation'. Example: Meera's class scores 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7 and 9 on a quiz. The mean is 5, the squared deviations add up to 32, the variance is 32 ÷ 8 = 4, and the SD is the square root of 4, which is 2 marks. Because it uses every score and ties in with the normal curve (about 68 per cent of scores fall within 1 SD of the mean), the SD is the most widely used measure of dispersion. Option c is right because the SD is defined as the square root of the variance. Exam trap: the average (mean) deviation also measures distance from the mean, but it averages absolute deviations instead of squaring them, so it is not derived from the variance.", "topic": "Standard deviation and variance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of the mean?", "options": {"a": "It is affected by the extreme scores", "b": "It minimizes the sum of squared deviations", "c": "The sum of deviations about the mean is zero", "d": "It is best used with ordinal data"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: 'it is best used with ordinal data' is not a characteristic of the mean. The arithmetic mean (M = ΣX ÷ N) is the sum of the scores divided by their number. It has three classic properties. First, it is the balance point of the distribution: deviations above and below it cancel, so the sum of deviations about the mean is always zero. Second, the sum of squared deviations about the mean is smaller than about any other value, the least-squares property that makes the mean the basis of variance, SD and regression. Third, because it uses every score, it is affected by extreme scores: the marks 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8, but adding one score of 40 lifts it to about 13.3. The mean assumes equal distances between scores, so it needs interval or ratio data. Ordinal data, such as class ranks or finishing positions in a race, show only order; the gap between first and second need not equal the gap between second and third, so the median is the proper average, and for nominal data the mode. Example: if Arjun's teacher records only class ranks, the median rank is a fair summary, but a mean rank assumes that the gap between 1st and 2nd equals the gap between 9th and 10th, which ranks cannot tell us. Option d is right because ordinal data suit the median, not the mean. Exam trap: the item asks for what is NOT true; options a, b and c are all genuine properties, so a hurried reader picks the first true statement.", "topic": "Properties of the mean", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Ratios measured by absolute variation for each observation are considered as:", "options": {"a": "Non-relative measures", "b": "Relative measures", "c": "High uniform measures", "d": "Low uniform measures"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: ratios formed from absolute variation are relative measures of dispersion. Measures of dispersion come in two kinds. Absolute measures (range, quartile deviation, mean deviation and standard deviation) are expressed in the same units as the data: marks, centimetres, rupees. They show how spread out one set of data is, but they cannot fairly compare sets measured in different units, or sets with very different averages. Relative measures solve this by dividing an absolute measure by an average or a related value, which cancels the units and leaves a pure number or percentage. Each absolute measure has a relative partner: coefficient of range = (highest minus lowest) ÷ (highest plus lowest); coefficient of quartile deviation = (Q3 minus Q1) ÷ (Q3 plus Q1); coefficient of mean deviation = mean deviation ÷ the average it was taken from; and coefficient of variation = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Example: Kabir's shop averages Rs 5,000 a day in sales with an SD of Rs 500, while his sister's averages Rs 20,000 with an SD of Rs 2,000; both coefficients of variation are 10 per cent, so their relative variability is the same. Option b is right because a ratio built from absolute variation is, by definition, a relative measure. Exam trap: 'non-relative' (option a) tempts because the stem mentions absolute variation, but the absolute measure is only the raw ingredient; once it is turned into a ratio, it becomes relative. Key note: the stem is garbled as printed ('ratios measured by absolute variation for each observation'); read as ratios of absolute variation to an average, it describes relative measures, so b stands.", "topic": "Absolute and relative dispersion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "_____ is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even though they are measured in different units.", "options": {"a": "Range", "b": "Standard deviation", "c": "Coefficient of variation", "d": "Mean deviation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the coefficient of variation (CV) is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even when they are measured in different units. Karl Pearson (1896) defined it as the standard deviation expressed as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Because the SD and the mean are in the same units, the units cancel and the CV is a pure percentage. That allows comparisons the SD cannot make: heights in centimetres against weights in kilograms, or the spread of marks in a class whose mean is 30 against one whose mean is 80. The set with the lower CV is the more consistent (more uniform, less variable). Example: in Riya's class, height has a mean of 160 cm and an SD of 8 cm (CV = 5 per cent), while weight has a mean of 50 kg and an SD of 5 kg (CV = 10 per cent); weight varies relatively twice as much, even though 8 looks larger than 5. Because it divides by the mean, the CV strictly needs ratio data with a true zero. Option c is right because it is the standard relative, unit-free measure of dispersion. Exam trap: the standard deviation is the best-known measure of spread, so it attracts guesses; but it is stated in the original units, so comparing an SD in centimetres with one in kilograms is meaningless.", "topic": "Coefficient of variation", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2017": [{"q": "Each one of us has a perception of one's own personality traits and this perception influences our behaviour. Psychologists call this perception as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the perception each of us holds of our own personality traits, which then guides how we act, is called the self-concept. The self-concept is the organised set of beliefs a person holds about who they are: traits (\"I am shy\"), roles (\"I am a daughter, a student\") and abilities (\"I am good at maths\"). It is descriptive and cognitive, an answer to the question \"Who am I?\". William James (1890) first split the self into the knower (the I) and the known (the Me). Carl Rogers (1959) then put the self-concept at the centre of his personality theory: when the self-concept clashes with actual experience, the person is in a state of incongruence and feels anxious, and a wide gap between the self-concept and the ideal self (who one wishes to be) signals poor adjustment. The self-concept also steers behaviour. Meera sees herself as \"not a public speaker\", so she never volunteers in class, and the avoidance then confirms her belief. Option c is right because the stem describes a perception of one's own traits, which is a description of the self, not a judgement of its worth. Exam trap: self-esteem is the evaluative, feeling part of the self (how much one likes or values oneself), measured by Morris Rosenberg's (1965) ten-item scale. Examiners swap the two terms, so remember: self-concept is \"who I am\", self-esteem is \"how I feel about who I am\".", "traps": {"a": "Self-esteem is the evaluative and emotional part of the self, how worthy or capable a person feels (as on Morris Rosenberg's 1965 scale); the stem describes a perception of traits, not a judgement of worth.", "b": "'Inner concept' is not a technical term in personality psychology; it is a filler built to sound like self-concept.", "d": "Self-evaluation is the act of judging oneself against a standard or against other people (as in Leon Festinger's 1954 social comparison theory); it is a process that feeds self-esteem, not the stored perception of one's traits."}}, {"q": "Which among the following traits was theorized by Maslow as being possessed by self-actualizers?", "concept": "The answer is d: an unhostile, philosophical sense of humour is one of the traits Abraham Maslow (1954) listed for self-actualising people. Self-actualisation is the top of Maslow's hierarchy of needs: becoming everything one is capable of becoming. In Motivation and Personality, Maslow described people he judged to have reached it, living and historical figures such as Abraham Lincoln, Eleanor Roosevelt and Albert Einstein, and found a shared set of traits. They include efficient perception of reality; acceptance of self, others and nature; spontaneity; a focus on problems outside themselves (problem-centred rather than ego-centred); detachment and a need for privacy; autonomy; fresh appreciation of ordinary things; peak experiences; Gemeinschaftsgefühl (a feeling of kinship with all humanity); a few deep friendships; a democratic character; a firm ethical sense; creativity; resistance to enculturation; and a philosophical, unhostile sense of humour. Their jokes poke gentle fun at human folly in general, including their own, rather than hurting or belittling a particular person. Kabir, who laughs at his own absent-mindedness instead of mocking a colleague's accent, shows this kind of humour. Option d is right because it appears, almost word for word, in Maslow's list. Exam trap: options that sound like general \"success\" traits, such as high intelligence or sociability, are planted because students assume self-actualisers must be brilliant or outgoing. Maslow stressed psychological health, acceptance and autonomy instead, and his self-actualisers actually valued solitude.", "traps": {"a": "Superior intelligence is not on Maslow's list; his traits describe psychological health and growth, not intellectual ability.", "b": "Egocentrism is the opposite of what Maslow found: self-actualisers are problem-centred, devoted to a task or mission outside themselves, not ego-centred.", "c": "Extraversion is a trait dimension from Carl Jung (1921) and Hans Eysenck, not a Maslow characteristic; self-actualisers showed detachment and a need for privacy, and kept a few deep friendships rather than many."}}, {"q": "The criticism levelled against behavioural theories is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the standard criticism of behavioural theories of personality is their limited recognition of temperament, emotion and subjective (inner) factors. Behavioural or learning theories see personality as the sum of learned behaviour patterns. B. F. Skinner (1953) explained behaviour by a person's history of reinforcement and refused to posit an inner self; John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950) translated Sigmund Freud's (1900) ideas into drive, cue, response and reward; Albert Bandura (1977) added learning by observation and expectations. Textbooks credit this family with real strengths: its concepts are precise and testable in the laboratory, and it draws attention to the situations and conditions under which behaviour occurs. The weakness is what it leaves out. Inborn temperament (some babies are calm, others irritable from birth), feelings, and the person's private experience of the world are treated as by-products or ignored, so the person can look like an empty box steered by the environment. Arjun has been shy in every setting since infancy, despite years of praise for speaking up; a strict learning account struggles to explain such a steady temperament. Option b is right because it names exactly this neglect of biology and inner life. Exam trap: options a and c are the classic criticisms of Freud's psychoanalysis, whose concepts are hard to test and which explains behaviour only after the fact (the charge Karl Popper (1963) pressed). Examiners move them onto behaviourism, but behaviourism has the opposite problem: it is testable but narrow.", "traps": {"a": "Inability to test or verify concepts is the main charge against Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (repression, the Oedipus complex); behavioural concepts such as reinforcement are defined operationally and tested in the laboratory, so testability is their strength.", "c": "Explaining behaviour only after the fact is also a criticism of psychoanalysis, which can account for any outcome once it has happened but predicts little; behaviourists, by contrast, predict and control behaviour from known reinforcement schedules.", "d": "Emphasis on the conditions under which behaviour occurs is a strength of behavioural theories, since it points to situations that can be changed in therapy; it is praise, not criticism."}}, {"q": "Who among the following would most likely use the adjective checklist?", "concept": "The answer is d: a trait theorist is the one most likely to use an adjective checklist. An adjective checklist is a list of descriptive words (anxious, friendly, stubborn, energetic and so on) on which the person, or someone who knows them well, ticks every word that applies. The best known is the Adjective Check List of Harrison Gough and Alfred Heilbrun (1965), with 300 adjectives scored on scales such as achievement, dominance and self-confidence. The method rests on the trait approach: personality can be described by stable, general dispositions, and the important ones are coded as words in everyday language. Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) combed an English dictionary and found about 18,000 such words. This lexical hypothesis led Raymond Cattell (1946) to his 16 source traits and later researchers to the Big Five. Riya ticks \"organised\", \"punctual\" and \"careful\" for herself; a trait psychologist reads that pattern as high conscientiousness. Option d is right because ticking trait adjectives is a direct way of placing a person on trait dimensions. Exam trap: the Q-sort also uses descriptive statements and looks similar, but it is the humanistic tool that Carl Rogers (1954) used to compare the real self with the ideal self, so do not pick humanistic just because self-description is involved.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviouristic theorists measure what a person does in specific situations, through direct observation, behavioural assessment and functional analysis, rather than asking for general trait words.", "b": "Humanistic theorists such as Carl Rogers prefer the Q-sort, interviews and reports of the person's own experienced self (real versus ideal self), not a checklist of trait adjectives.", "c": "Psychodynamic theorists look for unconscious material through free association, dream analysis and projective tests such as the Rorschach and the Thematic Apperception Test, which a conscious adjective checklist cannot reach."}}, {"q": "The _____ begins with puberty and ends with a mature capacity for love and realization of full adult sexuality.", "concept": "The answer is c: the genital stage begins at puberty and ends in a mature capacity for love and full adult sexuality. Sigmund Freud (1905) proposed that the libido (sexual energy) passes through a fixed series of stages, each except latency centred on a body zone: oral (birth to about 1), anal (about 1 to 3), phallic (about 3 to 6), latency (about 6 to puberty) and genital (puberty onward). In the genital stage the sexual drive, quiet during latency, reawakens with the hormones of puberty and is again centred on the genitals. Unlike the self-centred pleasure of the earlier stages, it is now directed outward to a partner, so the young adult can form mature, mutual love relationships and do productive work. Freud is reported to have summed up healthy adulthood as the ability \"to love and to work\" (lieben und arbeiten). How well a person manages this depends on how much libido stayed fixated at earlier stages. When Arjun, at 19, moves from passing crushes to a caring, committed relationship while settling into his studies, Freud would say he is working through the genital stage. Option c is right because it is the only stage that starts at puberty and has adult love as its goal. Exam trap: the Oedipus complex sits inside the phallic stage; it is a conflict, not a stage, so it cannot begin at puberty. The name Electra complex was coined by Carl Jung (1913), and Freud himself rejected it.", "traps": {"a": "The latency stage runs from about 6 to puberty; sexual impulses lie dormant and energy goes into school, sport and same-sex friendships, so it ends where the genital stage begins.", "b": "The Oedipus complex (boys) and the so-called Electra complex (girls) are the central conflict of the phallic stage, around 3 to 6, when the child desires the opposite-sex parent and later identifies with the same-sex parent; it is a conflict, not a stage starting at puberty.", "d": "The phallic stage (about 3 to 6) focuses pleasure on the genitals in early childhood and contains the Oedipal conflict; it ends with identification and the start of latency, long before puberty."}}, {"q": "Which of the following techniques was used by R.B. Cattell to identify 16 source traits as basic factors of personality?", "concept": "The answer is a: Raymond Cattell (1946, 1949) arrived at his 16 source traits by factor analysis. Factor analysis is a statistical technique, first developed by Charles Spearman (1904) for intelligence, that takes a large table of correlations among many measures and finds the smaller number of underlying factors that explain why certain measures go together. Cattell applied it to three kinds of data: L-data (life records, such as school reports and ratings by others), Q-data (questionnaire answers) and T-data (behaviour on objective tests). Clusters of traits that simply occur together in observable behaviour he called surface traits; the deeper factors that cause these clusters he called source traits. Repeated factor analyses gave 16 source traits, such as warmth, emotional stability, dominance and abstractedness, which he measured with the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). Hans Eysenck (1947) used the same method but settled on only a few broad dimensions. For example, if Meera's ratings on \"talkative\", \"sociable\" and \"outgoing\" always rise and fall together, factor analysis would extract one factor beneath them. Option a is right because factor analysis is the method that turned Cattell's many measures into 16 factors. Exam trap: correlation is tempting because factor analysis starts from correlations, but a correlation only links two variables at a time; it is the raw input, not the technique that extracts factors.", "traps": {"b": "Correlation measures how strongly two variables go together; it supplies the matrix that factor analysis works on, but by itself it cannot reduce dozens of measures to 16 underlying factors.", "c": "Regression predicts one variable (a criterion) from one or more others, as in predicting job success from test scores; it does not search for hidden common factors.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because factor analysis, option a, is exactly the technique Cattell used."}}, {"q": "Allport emphasized which of the following in his study of personality?", "concept": "The answer is b: Gordon Allport (1937) emphasised the normal, healthy, mature person in his study of personality. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, he argued that theories built on patients, such as the psychoanalysis of Sigmund Freud (1900), say little about healthy adults. He held that the mature personality is not ruled by childhood conflicts: adult motives become independent of the experiences in which they began, which he called functional autonomy. In Pattern and Growth in Personality (1961) he listed six criteria of maturity: extension of the sense of self, warm relating to others, emotional security and self-acceptance, realistic perception and commitment to work, self-objectification (insight with humour), and a unifying philosophy of life. Allport also stressed the uniqueness of each person and favoured the idiographic approach, the intensive study of one individual, for example through personal documents such as letters (Letters from Jenny, 1965). Kabir once studied only to please his father and now teaches because he loves it; Allport would see a healthy adult whose present motives drive him, not his past. Option b is right because Allport built his theory around the psychologically healthy adult rather than the neurotic patient. Exam trap: Allport is a trait theorist, so students link him with factor analysis, but he was critical of building personality out of factor analysis, which is the method of Raymond Cattell (1946) and Hans Eysenck (1947).", "traps": {"a": "Factor analytic techniques are the hallmark of Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck; Allport doubted that statistical factors averaged over groups could capture the living individual.", "c": "Group characteristics belong to the nomothetic approach, which compares many people on common dimensions; Allport favoured the idiographic study of the unique individual, even though he accepted common traits.", "d": "Cultural influences on personality were stressed by the neo-Freudians Karen Horney and Erich Fromm, not by Allport's trait psychology."}}, {"q": "Individual psychology of Adler can be considered as:", "concept": "The answer is a: individual psychology, the school Alfred Adler (1912) founded after leaving the Vienna Psychoanalytic Society of Sigmund Freud (1900) in 1911, is considered an optimistic theory of human nature. Adler saw each person as an indivisible whole (hence \"individual\") who is goal-directed and creative. Everyone begins life with feelings of inferiority, simply because a child is small and dependent, and these feelings push us to compensate and to strive for superiority, which in healthy people becomes striving for success and perfection. Behaviour is pulled by future goals (fictional finalism) more than pushed by the past, and each person creates a style of life. The healthy direction is social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl), concern for the welfare of others. Because Adler believed people can choose and change their goals, personality textbooks place him at the optimistic, free-choice, social end of their dimensions, in contrast to Freud. Riya, teased as a child for stammering, trains as a debater and later coaches shy children; Adler would see inferiority turned into healthy striving and social interest. Option a is right because Adler's view of human nature is hopeful and stresses free choice and growth. Exam trap: pessimism and determinism are features of Freud's view, in which behaviour is fixed by unconscious instincts and early childhood, and examiners offer them as look-alikes. Key note: Adler is often grouped with the neo-Freudians, such as Karen Horney (1937) and Erich Fromm (1941), so d is also a true label for him. But the stem asks how his theory views human nature, and the standard textbook answer is optimistic, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Pessimistic describes Sigmund Freud's view, in which people are driven by sexual and aggressive instincts and caught in lasting conflict; Adler saw people as able to grow and cooperate.", "c": "Deterministic also fits Freud, for whom behaviour is fixed by unconscious forces and early experience; Adler stressed creative power and free choice of one's goals and style of life.", "d": "Neo-Freudian is a historical grouping of theorists who broke from Freud and stressed social factors, and Adler is often placed in it; but it names a school, not a view of human nature, which is what the stem asks."}}, {"q": "According to statistical approach to abnormality, abnormal is defined as one who shows:", "concept": "The answer is c: under the statistical approach, abnormal means deviating from the typical or average pattern of behaviour. This criterion, also called statistical infrequency or statistical deviance, treats any trait or behaviour as abnormal if it is rare in the population. Many human characteristics fall roughly on a normal (bell-shaped) curve, so a common cut-off is a score more than about 2 standard deviations from the mean, which marks off roughly the most extreme 2 per cent at each end. Intelligence testing uses this logic: an IQ below about 70 is one part of the definition of intellectual disability. The strength of the approach is that it is objective and easy to measure. Its weakness is that rarity is not the same as disorder. Meera, with an IQ of 145, is just as statistically deviant as someone with an IQ of 55, yet nobody would call her gift a disorder; and common problems such as mild anxiety are not rare at all. For this reason clinicians combine several criteria, often summed up as the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Option c is right because it is the only option defined by distance from the average rather than by suffering or impairment. Exam trap: the other options are real signs of abnormality, but they belong to other criteria (personal distress, impairment, psychotic loss of reality), so match the option to the approach named in the stem.", "traps": {"a": "Loss of contact with reality (hallucinations, delusions) is the mark of psychosis and is judged under criteria such as maladaptiveness or irrationality, not by statistical rarity.", "b": "Being unhappy, withdrawn and depressed is the personal distress criterion, which judges abnormality by the person's suffering, whether or not the behaviour is rare.", "d": "Being disabled by anxiety is the dysfunction (impairment) criterion: behaviour is abnormal when it stops a person meeting daily demands at work, home or school."}}, {"q": "Sample of individuals who bear the diagnosis in question are referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is c: individuals who have the diagnosis being studied are called both probands and index cases, because the two terms mean the same thing. In family, twin and adoption studies of mental disorders, researchers start with people who already have the disorder (for example, patients with schizophrenia) and then examine their relatives to see how often the same disorder appears among them. The affected starting person is the proband, also called the index case, index patient or propositus. The relatives' rate of illness is then compared with the rate among relatives of healthy control probands. If risk rises with genetic closeness, higher in identical than in fraternal twins and higher in first-degree than in second-degree relatives, a genetic contribution is likely. For example, if Arjun is being treated for bipolar disorder and the research team then interviews his parents, siblings and cousins, Arjun is the proband and his relatives are the people whose risk is measured. \"Proband\" comes from the Latin probandus, \"one to be tested\", and in epidemiology the same term, \"index case\", names the first identified case in an outbreak. So a is a correct name, b is a correct name, and c combines them. Option c is right because probands and index cases are two names for the same affected individuals, so choosing only one would leave out an equally correct label. Exam trap: students pick a alone because proband is the usual textbook term, but index case is a standard synonym, not a different group such as the relatives or the controls.", "traps": {"a": "Proband is correct, but picking it alone misses that index case is an accepted synonym for the same affected person, so the fuller answer is c.", "b": "Index case is correct, but picking it alone leaves out proband, the term most genetics and psychopathology texts use for the same affected person.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because both listed terms name the diagnosed individuals through whom a family is studied; option c is right."}}, {"q": "Excessive anxiety and worry occurring more days than not for at least six months about a number of events or activities is the diagnostic criteria of which disorder?", "concept": "The answer is b: excessive anxiety and worry on more days than not for at least six months, about a number of events or activities, is criterion A for generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013). In GAD the worry is free-floating: it moves from health to money to work to family, and the person finds it hard to control. DSM-5 also requires at least three of six symptoms in adults (only one in children): restlessness or feeling keyed up, being easily tired, difficulty concentrating or the mind going blank, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep. The worry must cause real distress or impairment and must not be better explained by a drug, a medical condition or another disorder. The six-month rule separates GAD from ordinary stress reactions; DSM-III (1980) required only one month, and DSM-III-R (1987) raised it to six. Kabir, a bank clerk, has worried nearly every day for nine months about his job, his mother's health and whether he locked the door; he sleeps badly and has a stiff, aching neck. This is the classic GAD picture. Option b is right because the stem quotes the GAD criterion: the six-month duration and the many topics of worry. Exam trap: specific phobia also uses a six-month rule in DSM-5, so do not choose it on duration alone; phobic fear is tied to one object or situation, whereas GAD worry spreads across many things.", "traps": {"a": "Depression (major depressive disorder) needs depressed mood or loss of interest for at least 2 weeks, with symptoms such as sleep and appetite change and guilt; worry is not its core feature.", "c": "Post-traumatic stress disorder requires exposure to a traumatic event, followed by intrusions, avoidance, negative changes in mood and thinking, and hyperarousal lasting more than 1 month; the stem mentions no trauma.", "d": "Phobia (specific phobia) is marked fear of one particular object or situation, such as heights or dogs, with avoidance; the stem describes worry spread over many events."}}, {"q": "ERP is a treatment of choice for which disorder?", "concept": "The answer is b: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the treatment of choice for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). In OCD, obsessions (intrusive thoughts such as \"my hands are contaminated\") raise anxiety, and compulsions (washing, checking, counting) bring quick relief. That relief negatively reinforces the ritual, and it also stops the person learning that the anxiety would fade on its own. ERP breaks the cycle in two parts. In exposure, the client deliberately faces the feared cue, usually working up a graded hierarchy. In response prevention, the client refrains from the ritual. Anxiety rises, peaks and then falls through habituation and extinction, and the client learns that the feared disaster does not happen. Victor Meyer (1966) first described the method in London. Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986) explained how it works through emotional processing theory, and Foa's team showed in a 2005 trial that ERP outperformed the drug clomipramine. Guidelines now recommend ERP, alone or with an SSRI, as first-line care. Meera, who washes her hands 40 times a day, touches a doorknob and then waits an hour without washing, and repeats this daily until her anxiety barely rises. Option b is right because ERP was built to block compulsions, the core of OCD. Exam trap: ERP works by habituation and extinction, not by reciprocal inhibition; that principle belongs to Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation, which pairs relaxation with feared images.", "traps": {"a": "Depression is treated with Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, behavioural activation, interpersonal psychotherapy and antidepressants; there is no compulsion to prevent, so ERP is not its treatment of choice.", "c": "Mood disorders (depressive and bipolar) are treated with antidepressants, mood stabilisers such as lithium, and cognitive and interpersonal therapies, not exposure with ritual prevention.", "d": "Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotic drugs plus psychosocial work (family intervention, social skills training, CBT for psychosis); ERP targets compulsions, not psychotic symptoms."}}, {"q": "\"People with biological predisposition of difficulty controlling their emotions and raised in family environment that is invalidating, develop borderline personality disorder\", is an assumption of which theory?", "concept": "The answer is b: the idea that a biological difficulty in controlling emotions, combined with an invalidating family environment, produces borderline personality disorder (BPD) is the diathesis-stress (biosocial) theory of Marsha Linehan (1993). A diathesis-stress model says a disorder appears when an inborn vulnerability (the diathesis) meets environmental stress. In Linehan's version, the diathesis is emotional vulnerability: high sensitivity to emotional triggers, very intense reactions and a slow return to calm. The stress is an invalidating environment, in which a child's feelings are dismissed, punished or treated as wrong (\"stop crying, there is nothing to be upset about\"). Such a child never learns to name, trust or regulate emotions, and swings between bottling them up and extreme outbursts. This grows into the emotional instability, impulsive self-harm and stormy relationships of BPD. The theory led directly to dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), which balances acceptance of the client with training in skills for change: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Riya, a highly sensitive child whose parents mocked her tears, later cuts herself when overwhelmed; Linehan's model explains this as emotion dysregulation that an invalidating home never taught her to manage. Option b is right because the stem names both of her ingredients, the biological predisposition and the invalidating family. Exam trap: object relations theory also explains BPD by early family experience, so look for the words \"biological predisposition\" and \"invalidating\", which point to Linehan.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis explains disorders by unconscious conflicts from early psychosexual development; it does not combine a biological emotional vulnerability with an invalidating environment.", "c": "Object relations theory, especially Otto Kernberg's, explains BPD by splitting: the child fails to merge 'all good' and 'all bad' images of self and others, so later relationships swing between idealising and devaluing.", "d": "Cognitive theory (Aaron Beck and colleagues) explains BPD by rigid schemas such as 'I am bad' and 'the world is dangerous' and by all-or-nothing thinking, not by a biological diathesis plus invalidation."}}, {"q": "Which personality disorder is characterised by strong need to be the center of attention, inappropriate sexual and seductive behaviour and use of physical appearance to draw attention?", "concept": "The answer is b: a strong need to be the centre of attention, inappropriately seductive behaviour and the use of physical appearance to draw attention define histrionic personality disorder. It belongs to DSM-5 Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic group, along with antisocial, borderline and narcissistic personality disorders. Its old name was hysterical personality; DSM-III (1980) renamed it histrionic, from the Latin histrio, \"actor\". DSM-5 lists eight features, of which five are needed: discomfort when not the centre of attention; inappropriately seductive or provocative behaviour; rapidly shifting, shallow emotions; constant use of physical appearance to attract attention; impressionistic speech that lacks detail; theatrical, exaggerated expression of emotion; suggestibility; and seeing relationships as more intimate than they really are. The pattern begins by early adulthood and shows across many settings. Kabir arrives at every office meeting in flashy clothes, flirts with new colleagues, calls near-strangers his \"best friends\" and sulks whenever someone else is praised; his feelings are loud but change quickly. Option b is right because attention seeking, seductiveness and use of appearance are three of the eight histrionic criteria. Exam trap: narcissistic personality disorder also involves wanting attention, but the narcissist seeks admiration for a grandiose, superior self and lacks empathy, while the histrionic person wants any attention and gets it through emotional display and seduction.", "traps": {"a": "Borderline personality disorder is marked by instability of relationships, self-image and mood, frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, impulsivity and recurrent self-harm; attention seeking may occur but is not its defining feature.", "c": "Narcissistic personality disorder centres on grandiosity, a need for admiration, entitlement and lack of empathy; the person wants to be admired as special, not merely noticed through seductive display.", "d": "Antisocial personality disorder is a pervasive disregard for and violation of others' rights (deceit, aggression, lack of remorse) with conduct problems before 15, diagnosed only from 18; it does not involve seductive attention seeking."}}, {"q": "Severe, recurrent, temper outbursts, manifested verbally and/or behaviourally that are grossly out of proportion in intensity or duration to the situation or provocation, and inconsistent with developmental level are characteristics of which disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: severe, recurrent temper outbursts that are grossly out of proportion to the situation and inconsistent with developmental level are the core of disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (DMDD). DMDD was added in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) and placed among the depressive disorders, not the disruptive behaviour disorders. The outbursts may be verbal rages or physical aggression toward people or property, and occur on average three or more times a week. Between outbursts the mood is persistently irritable or angry most of the day, nearly every day. The pattern lasts 12 months or more and shows in at least two settings (home, school, with peers). Onset is before age 10, and the diagnosis is made only between ages 6 and 18. DMDD was created partly to curb a sharp rise in diagnoses of bipolar disorder in children. Research begun by Ellen Leibenluft (2003) on \"severe mood dysregulation\" found that chronically irritable children tend to grow into adults with depression and anxiety rather than bipolar disorder. Arjun, aged 8, screams and throws things several times a week over small requests and is cross and sullen in between, at home and at school: that is DMDD. Option c is right because the stem quotes the first DMDD criterion almost word for word. Exam trap: the irritability in DMDD is chronic, not episodic, and the outbursts are not manic episodes, which is exactly why DSM-5 separated it from bipolar disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Major depressive disorder needs at least 2 weeks of depressed mood or loss of interest with symptoms such as sleep, appetite and energy changes; irritability can replace sadness in children, but recurrent rage outbursts are not its criterion.", "b": "Dysthymia (persistent depressive disorder in DSM-5) is a long-lasting, low-grade depressed mood for at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children, where irritable mood may count); it is chronic low mood, not severe temper outbursts.", "d": "Bipolar disorder requires distinct episodes of mania or hypomania (elevated or irritable mood with more energy, less need for sleep, grandiosity); DMDD's irritability is chronic and non-episodic, which is why it was split off from bipolar disorder."}}, {"q": "MAO's are treatment of choice for which disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are classed as antidepressants, so depression is the disorder they are used for. Monoamine oxidase is an enzyme that breaks down the monoamine neurotransmitters serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine after their release. Blocking the enzyme leaves more of these transmitters available at the synapse, an observation that helped build the monoamine theory of depression. MAOIs were among the first modern antidepressants. In the early 1950s doctors noticed that iproniazid, a drug for tuberculosis, lifted patients' mood, and Nathan Kline (1957) promoted it as a \"psychic energizer\". Phenelzine and tranylcypromine followed. Their main danger is dietary: MAO in the gut normally destroys tyramine, found in aged cheese, cured meats and some wines, so a person on an MAOI who eats these foods can suffer a sudden, dangerous rise in blood pressure, the \"cheese reaction\". Because of this diet and the risk of drug interactions, MAOIs are now second or third line after the SSRIs, kept mainly for atypical or treatment-resistant depression. Kabir, who has not improved on two SSRIs, might be started on phenelzine and given a list of foods to avoid. Option c is right because MAOIs were discovered, developed and classed as antidepressants. Exam trap: \"MAO's\" in the stem means MAO inhibitors, the drugs, not the enzyme; and the anxiety options are planted because these drugs have some anti-anxiety effects. Key note: MAOIs also help panic disorder and social anxiety disorder, so a broad reading could favour an anxiety option. But they are classed and best known as antidepressants, so depression is the textbook answer, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Phobia (specific phobia) is treated first with exposure-based behaviour therapy, such as Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation or in vivo exposure; drugs play little part, and MAOIs are not its treatment of choice.", "b": "Generalised anxiety disorder is treated with CBT and with SSRIs, SNRIs, buspirone or short-term benzodiazepines; MAOIs are not a routine choice for it.", "d": "Obsessive-compulsive neurosis (OCD) responds to exposure and response prevention and to serotonergic drugs, the SSRIs and clomipramine; MAOIs are not the standard choice."}}, {"q": "On which principle is systematic desensitization based?", "concept": "The answer is b: systematic desensitisation is based on the principle of reciprocal inhibition. Joseph Wolpe (1958), in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition, borrowed the term from Charles Sherrington's (1906) work on spinal reflexes, where contracting one muscle inhibits its opposing muscle. Wolpe's version says that two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time: if a response that is incompatible with anxiety, usually deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of a feared stimulus, it weakens the bond between that stimulus and anxiety. The therapy has three steps. First, the client learns progressive muscle relaxation, based on the method of Edmund Jacobson (1929). Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, a list of feared situations ranked from least to most frightening, often rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS). Third, the relaxed client imagines each item in turn, moving up only when the current item no longer causes anxiety. The method extends Mary Cover Jones's (1924) treatment of Peter, whose fear of a rabbit faded as the rabbit was brought closer while he ate. Arjun, terrified of flying, relaxes while picturing booking a ticket, then driving to the airport, then take-off. Option b is right because the whole method rests on relaxation inhibiting anxiety. Exam trap: systematic desensitisation is gradual and pairs relaxation with feared images, unlike flooding, which exposes the client to the most feared stimulus at full strength; and it is counterconditioning, not cognitive restructuring.", "traps": {"a": "Restructuring of beliefs is the mechanism of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy, which identifies and tests distorted automatic thoughts; desensitisation changes a conditioned emotional response, not beliefs.", "c": "Countering irrational thoughts is the disputing step of Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (the ABCDE model), a cognitive method, not Joseph Wolpe's behavioural one.", "d": "Gaining insight into unconscious conflicts is the goal of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis; Joseph Wolpe held that anxiety is a learned habit that can be unlearned without any insight."}}, {"q": "Cindy, a nurse was at first extremely uncomfortable at the sight of the blood. After month of experience, working in an emergency room, she is no longer upset when confronted with serious injuries. Cindy has undergone:", "concept": "The answer is c: Cindy has undergone desensitisation, the weakening of an emotional response through repeated exposure to the stimulus that provoked it. At first the sight of blood made Cindy, a nurse, extremely uncomfortable. Working in an emergency room meant she met blood and serious injuries day after day, and nothing bad happened to her. With each exposure her fear reaction grew weaker, through habituation (a response fades when a stimulus is repeated without consequence) and extinction (a conditioned fear fades when the feared stimulus keeps appearing without the expected harm). After a month she could face serious injuries calmly. This is in vivo (real-life) exposure happening naturally on the job. In therapy the same process is used on purpose: Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitisation pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared images, and graded in vivo exposure takes the person through real situations step by step. The same principle explains why medical students who feel faint at their first dissection are calm a few weeks later. Option c is right because Cindy's distress declined only through repeated exposure to the feared stimulus, with no reinforcement of new behaviour and no removal from rewards. Exam trap: desensitisation describes a reduction of an emotional response; shaping and time-out are operant techniques that build or reduce a behaviour through its consequences, so they do not fit a change in feeling.", "traps": {"a": "Time-out is an operant procedure in which a person is removed from a reinforcing setting for a short period after misbehaviour (a form of negative punishment); nobody removed Cindy from anything.", "b": "Shaping is B. F. Skinner's method of reinforcing successive approximations to a target behaviour; Cindy was not rewarded step by step for new behaviour, her fear simply faded with exposure.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because desensitisation, option c, describes exactly what happened to Cindy."}}, {"q": "In rational-emotive behavior therapy:", "concept": "The answer is b: in rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT), clients learn to challenge their irrational beliefs. Albert Ellis (1955) developed the approach after finding that psychoanalytic insight rarely made his patients better. He first called it rational therapy; it later became rational-emotive therapy and, in 1993, REBT. Its core is the ABC model: an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional Consequence (C); the Belief (B) about the event does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands (\"I must succeed\", \"others must treat me fairly\"), awfulising (\"it would be terrible\"), low frustration tolerance (\"I can't stand it\") and self-downing. Therapy adds D, Disputing: the therapist actively questions the belief (Where is the evidence? Is it logical? Does it help?), and E, an Effective new belief that is flexible and realistic. Homework, role play and shame-attacking exercises turn the new belief into action. Meera fails a test and thinks, \"I must never fail; I am useless.\" Her therapist challenges the \"must\" and the label until she can say, \"I would prefer to pass; failing is unpleasant, and I can improve.\" Option b is right because disputing irrational beliefs is the defining technique of REBT. Exam trap: REBT is active and directive, unlike client-centred therapy; the therapist teaches and argues, so an option saying the client discovers rational insights on his own reverses Ellis's style.", "traps": {"a": "Taking responsibility for one's own choices is the central theme of existential therapy and of William Glasser's (1965) reality therapy; REBT agrees people are responsible for their beliefs, but this is not its defining feature.", "c": "Letting the client discover insights on his own is the non-directive style of Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy; Albert Ellis was openly active and directive, teaching and disputing rather than waiting for the client.", "d": "Making irrational unconscious elements conform to reality is the aim of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis ('where id was, there ego shall be'); REBT works on conscious beliefs in the present, not on the unconscious."}}, {"q": "According to Rogers' client-centered therapy, which is the important quality of a therapist?", "concept": "The answer is d: congruence, also called genuineness, is a key quality of the therapist in the client-centred therapy of Carl Rogers (1951). In a famous paper, Rogers (1957) set out the necessary and sufficient conditions for therapeutic personality change. Three of them concern the therapist and are known as the core conditions. Congruence means the therapist is real in the relationship: what they feel inside matches what they say and show, with no professional mask. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance of the client as a person, without conditions of worth. Accurate empathic understanding means sensing the client's private world \"as if\" it were one's own, without losing the \"as if\" quality, and communicating that understanding. Rogers believed that when a client experiences these conditions, the actualising tendency takes over and the client moves toward growth. If a counsellor, Kabir, feels bored but nods with fake enthusiasm, he is incongruent; if he honestly says, \"I notice I am losing the thread; can you help me see what matters most here?\", he is congruent. Option d is right because congruence is one of the three core therapist conditions. Exam trap: incongruity sounds similar but is the client's problem in Rogers's theory, the gap between self-concept and experience that therapy aims to close; and sympathy (feeling sorry for someone) is not empathy (feeling with them).", "traps": {"a": "Sympathy is feeling sorry for the client from the outside; Carl Rogers's condition is empathy, understanding the client's world from inside it, which is a different thing.", "b": "Open-mindedness is a general virtue in any helper but is not one of Carl Rogers's three core conditions (congruence, unconditional positive regard, empathy).", "c": "Incongruity (incongruence) is the client's problem in Rogers's theory, a mismatch between self-concept and experience that produces anxiety; the therapist must be the opposite, congruent."}}, {"q": "An approach that examines the clients' problems and then uses a variety of techniques that could help the clients are referred to as:", "concept": "The answer is b: the eclectic approach. Eclecticism in counselling and psychotherapy means first studying the particular client's problem and then choosing whichever techniques, from whichever school, are most likely to help with it, instead of applying one theory to everyone. Option b is right because the stem describes exactly this sequence: examine the problem, then use a variety of techniques. Arnold Lazarus (1967) argued for technical eclecticism and later built multimodal therapy (1976), which assesses seven areas summed up as BASIC I.D. (Behaviour, Affect, Sensation, Imagery, Cognition, Interpersonal relationships, Drugs and biology) and borrows a technique for each problem area, for example relaxation for tension and cognitive restructuring for self-critical thoughts. Technical eclecticism borrows techniques without their theories; psychotherapy integration goes further and fuses theories into a new framework (theoretical integration) or builds on the factors shared by all therapies (common factors). For example, a counsellor seeing Arjun for exam anxiety might teach him breathing and relaxation from behaviour therapy, challenge his 'I must top the class' belief with cognitive techniques, and use the empathic listening of Carl Rogers (1951) to build trust. Exam trap: resistance and transference sound like approaches, but they are psychoanalytic phenomena that occur inside therapy, and psychiatry is a medical profession, not a way of choosing techniques. Also keep eclecticism (picking from many schools) apart from integration (merging schools into one new model).", "traps": {"a": "Psychiatry is the branch of medicine that diagnoses and treats mental disorders, largely with medication; it is a profession, not a method of selecting techniques from several schools to fit a client's problem.", "c": "Resistance is the client's unconscious opposition to the work of therapy, a phenomenon Sigmund Freud described in psychoanalysis; the therapist interprets it, so it is not an approach that draws on many techniques.", "d": "Transference is the client's redirection of feelings from early figures such as parents onto the therapist, central to Freud's psychoanalysis; it is a process within one school, not a multi-technique approach."}}, {"q": "Nonmaleficence refers to:", "concept": "The answer is a: nonmaleficence means 'above all, do no harm'. The phrase echoes the Latin maxim primum non nocere, linked to the Hippocratic tradition in medicine, and it asks every helper to avoid actions that could injure a client physically, psychologically or socially, and to weigh the risks of an intervention before using it. Option a is right because nonmaleficence literally means not doing harm (Latin non, not, and maleficus, harmful). Karen Kitchener (1984) set out five moral principles for counselling ethics: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice and fidelity. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979) gave medicine four: respect for autonomy, nonmaleficence, beneficence and justice. The APA Ethics Code (2002) joins the first pair in Principle A, Beneficence and Nonmaleficence. In practice nonmaleficence means not using a technique you are untrained in, avoiding exploitative dual relationships, and not pushing a fragile client into a frightening task without preparation. For example, if Meera's counsellor has never been trained in trauma work, referring her to a trained colleague rather than experimenting on her honours nonmaleficence. Exam trap: nonmaleficence (avoid harm) and beneficence (actively do good) are a look-alike pair that examiners swap. Patience, kindness and self-care are good qualities in a counsellor, but none of them is the definition of a named ethical principle.", "traps": {"b": "Patience is a useful personal quality in a counsellor, but it is not the meaning of any named ethical principle; nonmaleficence is specifically the duty to avoid causing harm.", "c": "Self-care (helpers looking after their own health so that burnout does not impair their work) is encouraged in ethics codes as part of competence, but it is not the definition of nonmaleficence, which concerns not harming the client.", "d": "Kindness is closest to beneficence, the duty to actively do good for the client; nonmaleficence is the narrower duty of not causing harm."}}, {"q": "Providing equality of resources and opportunity for all people upholds the ethical principle of:", "concept": "The answer is c: providing equality of resources and opportunity for all people upholds the principle of justice. In professional ethics, justice means fairness: treating people equally unless there is a relevant reason to treat them differently, giving everyone fair access to services, and sharing benefits and burdens evenly regardless of caste, religion, gender, income or disability. Option c is right because 'equality of resources and opportunity' is the textbook wording of justice. It is one of the five principles for counselling set out by Karen Kitchener (1984) (autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, fidelity), one of the four principles of biomedical ethics of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979), and Principle D of the APA Ethics Code (2002), which asks psychologists to give all persons fair access to, and equal quality of, their services. In research, the Belmont Report (1979) applies justice through fair selection of participants, so that one group does not carry all the risks while another enjoys the benefits. For example, a college counselling centre that offers free sessions, a ramp for wheelchair users and counselling in Hindi as well as English, so that Kabir, a labourer's son, can use it as easily as anyone else, is acting on justice. Exam trap: the four ethics words in the options are easily swapped. Veracity is truthfulness, autonomy is the client's right to make their own choices, and fidelity is keeping promises and being trustworthy; only justice is about fairness and equal access.", "traps": {"a": "Veracity is the duty to be truthful and honest with clients, a principle later writers added to Kitchener's list; it concerns honesty, not the fair sharing of resources.", "b": "Autonomy is respect for the client's right to make free, informed choices about their own life and treatment; it concerns self-determination, not equal access for all.", "d": "Fidelity is faithfulness to commitments: keeping promises, honouring confidentiality and maintaining trust; it concerns loyalty to the client, not fairness across people."}}, {"q": "The stage of counselling in which client should reach clearer understanding of his or her life concerns and begin to formulate a new sense of hope and direction is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: in-depth exploration. A three-stage model of the counselling process, associated with Lewis Patterson and Elizabeth Welfel's The Counseling Process and repeated in many Indian texts, runs as follows. Stage one, initial disclosure, builds the relationship: the client tells the story, and the counsellor uses attending, listening and primary empathy to create trust. Stage two, in-depth exploration, goes deeper: the counsellor uses more challenging skills such as advanced empathy, immediacy and gentle confrontation, and the client looks at feelings, patterns and blind spots. The goal of this stage is that the client reaches a clearer understanding of his or her life concerns and begins to formulate a new sense of hope and direction. Stage three, commitment to action, decides what the client will actually do: concrete goals, a chosen plan and carrying it out. Option a is right because the stem repeats the goal of the second stage almost word for word: understanding, and only the first stirrings of direction, come before action. The model parallels the three stages of Gerard Egan's The Skilled Helper (first edition 1975), which later editions call the current picture, the preferred picture and the way forward, and the exploration, insight and action stages of Clara Hill and Karen O'Brien (1999). For example, by session four Riya, who came in feeling 'stuck', realises that her distress is about studying for a career her parents chose, and begins to feel she could decide for herself. She has completed in-depth exploration; drawing up steps to talk to her parents is commitment to action. Exam trap: the word 'direction' pulls students toward commitment to action, which is about specific goals, plans and doing. Key note: 'a new sense of direction' could be read as the start of commitment to action, but the model states this outcome as the goal of the second stage, and commitment to action is where that direction becomes goals and steps; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Commitment to action is the third stage, where the client sets concrete goals, chooses strategies and carries out a plan; the stem describes only beginning to formulate hope and direction, which comes before that.", "c": "Initial disclosure is the first stage, where rapport is built and the client tells the story; a clearer understanding of the concerns has not yet been reached.", "d": "None of the above does not fit, because the stem matches a listed stage: in-depth exploration (option a)."}}, {"q": "Concurrent validity refers to:", "concept": "The answer is d: concurrent validity is shown when scores on a test correlate highly with scores on an established measure of the same attribute taken at about the same time. Validity asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure. Criterion-related validity checks this against an outside standard, the criterion, such as a psychiatrist's diagnosis, a gold-standard scale or job ratings. It has two types that differ only in timing: in concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured together; in predictive validity the criterion comes later, as when entrance scores are correlated with first-year marks. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) discussed concurrent validity as one of the four types of validity named in the APA's 1954 technical recommendations, alongside predictive, content and construct validity. Option d is right because it describes a high correlation with another measure of the same attribute, which is how a new test is checked against an existing one. For example, Kabir writes a 20-item Hindi depression questionnaire, gives it to 100 clinic patients on the same day as the Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961), and finds r = .80: good concurrent validity. Exam trap: option d's wording, 'correlate with tests that measure the same attribute', also describes convergent validity, a part of construct validity; here it is the only option that involves correlating the test with another measure, so it is the answer. Do not let the word 'concurrent' pull you to option a, and do not confuse validity with the reliability ideas in options b and c.", "traps": {"a": "This is a lay reading of 'concurrent' (at the same time). Giving two tests together is only the timing of a concurrent validity study; the validity itself is the correlation between the test and the criterion.", "b": "Agreement between two or more clinicians or scorers is inter-rater (inter-scorer) reliability, measured with statistics such as Jacob Cohen's (1960) kappa; it concerns the consistency of judges, not validity.", "c": "Items relating consistently to one another is internal consistency reliability, estimated by split-half methods, KR-20 or Cronbach's alpha; it shows the items hang together, not that the test agrees with a criterion."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is assessed by Cronbach alpha?", "concept": "The answer is c: Cronbach's alpha assesses internal consistency. Internal consistency reliability is the degree to which the items of a test hang together, that is, whether they all measure the same underlying construct, judged from a single administration. Lee Cronbach (1951) generalised the KR-20 formula of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937), which suits right-or-wrong items, to items with several score points such as Likert ratings; for items scored 0 or 1, alpha equals KR-20. Alpha is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients, so it avoids the arbitrariness of choosing one split. It runs from 0 to 1; about .70 is often taken as acceptable and .80 to .90 as good, while very high values can signal redundant items. Alpha rises with the average inter-item correlation and with the number of items. Option c is right because alpha is computed from the item variances within one sitting, which is exactly what internal consistency means. For example, if Meera's 10-item exam-stress scale gives alpha = .85, her items are measuring one thing consistently. Exam trap: each option pairs with its own statistic. Concurrent validity uses a correlation with a criterion measured at the same time; inter-rater reliability uses Jacob Cohen's (1960) kappa or percentage agreement; test-retest reliability correlates scores from two occasions.", "traps": {"a": "Concurrent validity is a criterion-related validity, shown by correlating the test with an established criterion measured at the same time; alpha says nothing about agreement with an outside measure.", "b": "Inter-rater reliability is agreement between two or more scorers or judges, measured by Cohen's kappa or percentage agreement, not by alpha.", "d": "Test-retest reliability is the correlation between scores from two administrations separated in time (the coefficient of stability); alpha needs only one administration."}}, {"q": "The definition of psychological constructs such as love in a way as to allow measurement of it is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: an operational definition. An operational definition defines a construct by the operations used to measure or produce it, so that anyone can repeat the measurement. Love, anxiety or intelligence cannot be seen directly, so the researcher states exactly what will count as the variable: love is the score on the Love Scale of Zick Rubin (1970), hunger is hours without food, aggression is the number of times a child hits a Bobo doll in ten minutes. The idea, operationism, came from the physicist Percy Bridgman (1927) in The Logic of Modern Physics, and Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought it into psychology. Option c is right because the stem asks for a definition that allows measurement, which is the whole purpose of an operational definition; it makes research objective and replicable, though no single operation captures every side of a construct. For example, Arjun, studying romantic love among college couples, might operationally define love as the total score on Rubin's 13-item Love Scale, or as the seconds a couple spend looking into each other's eyes during a ten-minute conversation. Exam trap: a conceptual (constitutive) definition explains a construct in words using other concepts ('love is deep affection and attachment'), but it does not tell you how to measure it, so 'conceptualisation' is the near miss examiners offer.", "traps": {"a": "Conceptualisation is defining a construct in abstract words (a conceptual definition) and refining what it means; it comes before operationalisation and does not by itself make the construct measurable.", "b": "A scale of measurement is the level at which a variable is measured (nominal, ordinal, interval or ratio, in S. S. Stevens's 1946 scheme); it describes the numbers, not how a construct is defined so it can be measured.", "d": "Hypothesising is stating a testable prediction about the relation between variables; it uses operationally defined variables but is not the act of defining them."}}, {"q": "What is the difference between data measured on an interval scale and on a ratio scale?", "concept": "The answer is b: a ratio scale has a true zero point, so zero on the scale means none of the quantity being measured. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels. Nominal scales name categories (blood group, religion). Ordinal scales rank (position in class). Interval scales have equal distances between points, so differences are meaningful, but the zero is arbitrary: 0 degrees Celsius does not mean no temperature, and an IQ of 0 would not mean no intelligence. Ratio scales have equal intervals and an absolute zero, so ratios are meaningful: a reaction time of 400 milliseconds is truly twice one of 200, and 0 kg means no weight at all. Option b is right because the true zero is the one feature that separates ratio from interval; both have equal intervals. For example, Riya's score of 40 on an anxiety questionnaire does not make her 'twice as anxious' as Kabir with 20, because the questionnaire's zero is not an absence of anxiety, but if she takes 60 seconds to solve a puzzle and he takes 30, she really did take twice as long. Because ratio data have a true zero, every statistic, including the coefficient of variation and the geometric mean, can be used on them. Exam trap: option a is the same sentence with interval and ratio swapped. Remember the order NOIR (nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio): each level adds one property, and the R for ratio adds the real zero.", "traps": {"a": "This reverses the two scales: it is the ratio scale, not the interval scale, whose zero means a true absence of the quantity; an interval scale such as Celsius temperature has an arbitrary zero.", "c": "Putting data into categories describes a nominal scale; both interval and ratio scales measure quantities on a continuum, so this does not separate them.", "d": "Both interval and ratio scales have equal intervals between points (that is what lifts them above ordinal scales), so this statement is false."}}, {"q": "Rating scales are used to record judgements about:", "concept": "The answer is d: rating scales are used to record judgements about oneself, about other people and about objects. A rating scale asks a judge to place something on a set of ordered points, such as 1 to 5 or 'never' to 'always', so that an impression becomes a number. Formats include numerical, graphic (a line to mark), descriptive and behaviourally anchored scales, the summated agreement scale of Rensis Likert (1932) and the semantic differential of Charles Osgood (1957). Option d is right because each listed target is a standard use. About oneself: self-rating scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961), on which Meera rates her own sadness, sleep and appetite. About others: a teacher rates a pupil's attention, a supervisor rates an employee, a clinician rates a patient on the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (Max Hamilton, 1960), or a parent rates a child's behaviour. About objects: consumers rate products, judges rate paintings, and students rate an idea or a picture on a semantic differential (good to bad, strong to weak). Because human judges are involved, rating scales carry rater errors: the halo effect named by Edward Thorndike (1920), leniency and severity, and central tendency (avoiding the extremes). Exam trap: each single option is true, which is why examiners offer them; picking one alone ignores the other two uses. When every listed target is a genuine use, choose 'all of the above'.", "traps": {"a": "Self-rating is a real use (for example rating one's own mood on a depression inventory), but chosen alone it leaves out ratings of other people and of objects, which are equally standard.", "b": "Rating objects (products, pictures, ideas) is a real use, but chosen alone it leaves out self-ratings and ratings of other people.", "c": "Rating others (teachers rating pupils, clinicians rating patients) is the classic use, but chosen alone it leaves out self-ratings and ratings of objects."}}, {"q": "Projective techniques have been popular with clinical psychologists, their validity is considered _____ among tests of personality.", "concept": "The answer is c: although projective techniques have long been popular with clinical psychologists, their validity is generally considered the lowest among tests of personality. Projective tests, such as the inkblots of Hermann Rorschach (1921), the Thematic Apperception Test of Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), sentence completion and figure drawings, present an ambiguous stimulus and assume that people project their needs and conflicts into their answers. That makes faking harder and yields rich clinical material. The trouble is psychometric. Many techniques have several competing scoring systems, interpretation is often global and intuitive, norms are weak, retest reliability is poor because stories and percepts change, and even scored indices add little beyond an interview and an inventory (poor incremental validity). Scott Lilienfeld, James Wood and Howard Garb (2000), reviewing the evidence, found support for only a few Rorschach indices, such as those for thought disorder, and little for most others or for figure drawings. Loren Chapman and Jean Chapman (1967, 1969) showed why clinicians keep trusting invalid signs: illusory correlation. Option c is right because, compared with objective inventories such as the MMPI, which have the strongest validity data, projectives sit at the bottom. For example, if one clinician reads Kabir's TAT stories as 'aggressive', a second clinician using another system may disagree, and the label may not predict how he actually behaves. Exam trap: popularity is not validity; the stem's 'have been popular' sets up a contrast, not a clue that validity is high. The Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) improved Rorschach scoring reliability, but it did not lift projectives to the validity of inventories.", "traps": {"a": "The highest validity belongs to structured objective inventories such as the MMPI and the NEO, backed by large bodies of reliability and validity research; projective tests have the weakest validity record, not the strongest.", "b": "Moderate overstates the case: apart from a few scored indices (such as Rorschach signs of thought disorder), reviews find projective validity poor, which standard texts describe as the lowest among personality tests.", "d": "None of the above does not fit, because a listed option is right: the standard textbook rating of projective validity is lowest (option c)."}}, {"q": "In administration of Rorschach, the inquiry refers to that part of the examination in which:", "concept": "The answer is c: in the Rorschach, the inquiry is the part of the examination in which the cards are shown to the test taker for the second time. Hermann Rorschach (1921) published ten inkblots: five achromatic, two black and red, and three multicoloured. In the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) administration has two main phases. In the response phase (free association) the examiner hands over the cards one at a time in a fixed order, asks 'What might this be?', and records every response word for word without suggesting answers. In the inquiry phase, after all ten cards, the examiner goes back through each card and each response to learn where on the blot it was seen (location), what made it look that way, such as form, colour, shading or movement (determinants), and what it was (content), using non-leading questions such as 'What made it look like that?'. An optional testing-the-limits phase may follow, in which the examiner asks directly about popular responses or determinants the person never used. Option c is right because the inquiry depends on re-exposing the same cards so that each response can be scored. For example, if Riya said 'a bat' to Card I, in the inquiry the examiner hands her Card I again and asks her to show where the bat is and what made it look like one. Exam trap: option b, the first exposure, is the response phase; the inquiry always comes after it and never uses leading questions.", "traps": {"a": "Demographic and background information is collected in the interview before testing begins; it is not part of the Rorschach inquiry.", "b": "The first exposure of the cards is the response (free association) phase, in which the test taker says what each blot might be; the inquiry follows it.", "d": "The examiner answers the test taker's questions only briefly and non-directively (for instance 'It's up to you'); the inquiry is the examiner's structured questioning about location and determinants, not an open question period."}}, {"q": "Psychometric soundness of projective technique like RIB is difficult to evaluate because:", "concept": "The answer is b: the psychometric soundness of a projective technique such as the Rorschach Inkblot test is difficult to evaluate because traditional test-retest reliability procedures are not applicable. Reliability is normally checked in standard ways: test-retest gives the same test twice and correlates the scores, and split-half divides the items into two equivalent halves. The point comes from the assessment text of Ronald Cohen and Mark Swerdlik, and the reasons are easy to see. Responses are open and free, so they can change from one occasion to the next with the person's mood, recent experiences and fatigue (passing states, not only stable traits), and on a retest people may remember and repeat, or deliberately vary, their earlier answers. A change in responses therefore does not show unreliability in the usual sense. Split-half is also unsuitable, because the ten blots are not interchangeable items: each card has its own shape and colour and pulls different responses, so no two halves are equivalent. Option b is right because it names this reliability problem directly. For example, if Arjun sees threatening animals in the blots the day after a frightening exam result and gentler figures a month later, the change may reflect his state, not measurement error. Exam trap: option c reverses the problem; a test tapping enduring traits would suit test-retest, and the difficulty is that responses also reflect shifting states. Key note: in the study quiz for Cohen and Swerdlik's Psychological Testing and Assessment, option c reads 'taps enduring traits, not transient states' and is still not a reason for the difficulty, so d cannot be right; one could argue a as well, since construct validation of projectives is hard, but that quiz gives b, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Construct validation of projectives is indeed hard, but it is not the reason this textbook point gives; the stated difficulty is that standard reliability procedures, especially test-retest, do not fit open, state-sensitive responses.", "c": "This is garbled: if a projective tapped only stable, enduring traits, test-retest would work well; the real difficulty is that responses also reflect changing states.", "d": "All of the above fails because option c is false as worded, so the three cannot all be correct; only b states the accepted reason."}}, {"q": "To prevent ethical abuse in psychological research the APA has suggested that:", "concept": "The answer is a: to prevent ethical abuse in research, the APA asks psychologists to treat all participants with respect and concern for their dignity. The American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016) has five aspirational General Principles. Principle E, Respect for People's Rights and Dignity, covers privacy, confidentiality and self-determination, and the enforceable standards (mainly Standard 8, with Standard 4 on privacy and confidentiality) turn it into research rules: informed consent before the study, freedom to withdraw, confidentiality of data, minimising harm and debriefing afterwards. Option a is right because respect for participants' dignity is the umbrella principle from which these rules follow, and it holds without exceptions. The other options are distorted versions of real rules. Deception is not banned: Standard 8.07 allows it when the study has significant scientific value, no effective non-deceptive method is feasible, nobody is deceived about anything expected to cause physical pain or severe emotional distress, and the deception is explained in debriefing. Data are kept confidential, not made public. Harm must be minimised and, if it occurs, reasonable steps taken to reduce it, but there is no rule that every harm be corrected by counselling. For example, when Kabir takes part in a conformity study with a cover story, he is told the real aim at the end, may withdraw his data, and his answers are stored under a code number. Exam trap: absolute words ('must avoid', 'all', 'must be made public') mark the distorted options; the real APA rules on deception and harm are conditional.", "traps": {"b": "The APA does not ban deception; Standard 8.07 permits it under strict conditions (real value, no workable alternative, no severe distress, prompt debriefing), so 'must avoid deception' is too absolute.", "c": "The APA requires confidentiality: individual data are protected and reported only in coded or group form, so making all data public would breach the code.", "d": "Researchers must minimise harm and take reasonable steps to correct it, often through debriefing, but the code has no rule that all harm be corrected with counselling."}}, {"q": "Three major concerns for psychological researchers are:", "concept": "The answer is a: deception and lasting harm to subjects are two of the three major ethical concerns for psychological researchers; the third is invasion of privacy. Introductory psychology texts commonly sum up research ethics in these three worries. Deception means misleading participants about the purpose or procedures of a study (a cover story, a confederate, false feedback); it is allowed only when justified and followed by debriefing. Lasting harm means psychological, physical or social damage that outlives the study, such as lowered self-esteem, guilt or stress; participants should leave no worse off than they came. Invasion of privacy means collecting or revealing personal information without consent, or observing people where they expect privacy; confidentiality and anonymity guard against it. The obedience study of Stanley Milgram (1963) is the classic case: participants were deceived about the shocks and many showed intense stress, raising questions of deception and harm together. Option a is right because it is the option that holds two of the three named concerns. For example, if Riya secretly records classmates' phone chats for a study on gossip, she invades their privacy; if she also falsely tells them they failed an IQ test to watch their reactions, she uses deception that may cause lasting harm unless she debriefs them. Exam trap: falsified results is serious research misconduct, but it is the researcher's dishonesty, not a concern about how participants are treated. Key note: as printed, the item looks like a three-part list (deception, lasting harm, invasion of privacy) split across options a and d; a holds two of the three named concerns, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Loss of future research possibilities is a practical worry for researchers (for example, participants becoming suspicious of psychologists), not one of the three ethical concerns about how participants are treated.", "c": "Falsified results is research misconduct by the researcher (fabricating or falsifying data); it is unethical, but it is not one of the three concerns about protecting participants.", "d": "Invasion of privacy is the third of the three concerns, but on its own it leaves out deception and lasting harm; option a carries two of the three and is the one that answers the stem."}}, {"q": "If groups of participants are selected to represent sub-groups in the population (e.g. such as selecting entire class of psychology students to be compared to a group of history students). This is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: cluster sampling. In cluster sampling the researcher selects whole, naturally occurring groups (clusters) such as classes, schools, villages or hospital wards, usually at random, and studies everyone, or a random sample, inside each chosen group. Only a list of clusters is needed, not a list of every individual, which makes it practical for large or scattered populations; when the clusters are geographical areas it is also called area sampling. Option a is right because the stem's cue is 'selecting an entire class': the unit chosen is an intact group, not individual students. The cost is precision. Ideally each cluster is a mixed miniature of the population, but in practice people in one class or village tend to be alike, so sampling error is larger than for a simple random sample of the same size. Compare stratified sampling, where the population is first divided into strata (such as gender or year of study) and individuals are drawn at random from every stratum, so that each subgroup is represented. For example, to compare the attitudes of psychology and history students, Meera takes all 40 students of one psychology section and all 40 of one history section, rather than picking individuals from the whole university list. Exam trap: clusters and strata are easily swapped. In stratified sampling you sample individuals within every stratum; in cluster sampling you sample whole groups and take them intact. Key note: the phrase 'to represent sub-groups' sounds like stratified sampling, but stratified sampling is not offered, and taking an entire intact class is the defining move of cluster sampling, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Simple random sampling gives every individual an equal and independent chance of selection (lottery or random numbers); it picks individuals, not whole classes.", "c": "Haphazard sampling takes whoever happens to be met, with no plan or randomisation; it does not involve deliberately selecting intact groups.", "d": "Opportunity (convenience) sampling uses whoever is available at the time, such as the students sitting in the canteen; it selects people by convenience, not entire groups such as a whole class."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a benefit of using simple random sampling?", "concept": "The answer is a: a benefit of simple random sampling is that the accuracy of the results can be calculated. In simple random sampling every member of the population has an equal and independent chance of being chosen, using the lottery (fishbowl) method, a table of random numbers or a computer. Because selection follows known probability rules, statistical theory tells us how far a sample figure is likely to differ from the true population value: the standard error can be computed, and from it a margin of error and a confidence interval. Option a is right because this ability to estimate sampling error is the main advantage of probability sampling over non-probability methods such as quota or convenience sampling, where the size of the error cannot be known. Random selection also removes the researcher's personal bias in choosing people. For example, if Arjun randomly selects 400 of a college's 4,000 students and finds that 30 percent report poor sleep, he can state that the true figure very probably lies within about 4 to 5 percentage points of 30 percent (a 95 percent confidence interval). Exam trap: random sampling makes a representative sample likely, not certain; any single random sample can, by bad luck, miss a small subgroup, so 'always representative' is the overstatement examiners plant.", "traps": {"b": "Random selection makes representativeness likely and its error measurable, but never guaranteed; by chance a single random sample can over- or under-represent a group, so 'always' is false.", "c": "Interviewers choosing respondents freely describes quota sampling, a non-probability method that lets interviewer bias in; simple random sampling removes that choice.", "d": "Informants refusing to take part is non-response, a problem for any sampling method because it can bias the sample; it is not a benefit of random sampling."}}, {"q": "In the equation of straight line Y = mX + c, if m is equal to zero then:", "concept": "The answer is b: if m = 0 in Y = mX + c, then as X increases, Y remains constant. In the equation of a straight line, m is the slope (gradient): the change in Y for every one-unit increase in X. c is the Y-intercept, the value of Y where the line crosses the Y axis, that is, when X = 0. If m is positive the line rises (as X increases, Y increases); if m is negative it falls (as X increases, Y decreases); if m = 0 the equation becomes Y = 0 x X + c, so Y = c for every value of X and the line is horizontal. Option b is right because a zero slope means a change in X brings no change in Y. In statistics the same line is written Y = a + bX in simple linear regression, the method that grew out of the work of Francis Galton (1886) on regression to the mean, where b is the regression coefficient (the slope). A zero slope there means the predictor tells you nothing about the criterion, and the correlation between them is zero. For example, if Kabir's exam marks stay at 60 however many hours (X) he spends listening to music, the line is Y = 60: m = 0 and c = 60. Exam trap: option c describes a negative relation stated from Y's side, which needs m less than zero, not m = 0. Also do not mix up m and c: c = 0 would only make the line pass through the origin.", "traps": {"a": "As X increases Y decreases describes a negative slope (m less than 0), a line falling from left to right, not a zero slope.", "c": "Y increasing while X decreases is again a negative relation, just stated from Y's side; it needs m less than 0, not m = 0.", "d": "As X increases Y increases describes a positive slope (m greater than 0), a line rising from left to right; with m = 0, Y does not change at all."}}, {"q": "What is the other name of Chi square goodness of fit test?", "concept": "The answer is d: none of these, because the chi-square goodness-of-fit test is also called the one-sample (one-way, or one-variable) chi-square test. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test for frequency data, where people are counted into categories rather than given scores. It compares the observed frequency (O) in each category with the frequency expected (E) under the null hypothesis: chi-square = sum of (O - E) squared / E. The goodness-of-fit test uses one categorical variable and asks whether its frequencies match a hypothesised pattern, such as equal preference or known population proportions; its degrees of freedom are k - 1 for k categories. The chi-square test of independence uses two variables in a contingency table and asks whether they are associated; its degrees of freedom are (rows - 1) x (columns - 1). Option d is right because none of the three listed names refers to the goodness-of-fit test. For example, Riya asks 120 students which of four chai brands they prefer; equal preference predicts 30 each. She observes 45, 30, 25 and 20, so chi-square = (225 + 0 + 25 + 100) / 30 = 11.67 with 3 degrees of freedom. That exceeds the .05 critical value of 7.81, so the preferences are not equal. Exam trap: 'two-sample chi-square' sounds like the partner of a one-sample test, but the chi-square that involves two variables is the test of independence (or homogeneity), not goodness of fit.", "traps": {"a": "There is no standard test called chi-square ANOVA: analysis of variance compares group means with the F ratio, whereas chi-square compares category frequencies.", "b": "The two-sample (two-variable) chi-square is the test of independence or homogeneity on a contingency table; goodness of fit involves only one sample and one variable.", "c": "The Wilcoxon tests (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945) are rank-based non-parametric tests, the signed-rank test for paired scores and the rank-sum test for two independent groups; they compare ranks, not observed and expected frequencies."}}, {"q": "As the sample size increases the shape of the t-distribution changes to:", "concept": "The answer is d: as sample size increases, the t distribution becomes more and more normal looking. William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, published the distribution under the pen name 'Student', hence Student's t. It is used when the population standard deviation is unknown and must be estimated from the sample, which adds extra uncertainty. The t distribution is a family of curves, one for each number of degrees of freedom (df = n - 1 for one sample). Like the normal curve, each is symmetrical and bell-shaped around zero; unlike it, a t curve has a lower peak and heavier tails, because small samples give unsteady estimates of the standard deviation. As df rise the estimate steadies, the tails thin and the curve closes in on the standard normal (z) curve; by about 30 df the two are very close, and with infinite df they are identical. That is why the two-tailed .05 critical value falls from 12.71 at df = 1 to 2.04 at df = 30, and towards 1.96 beyond. Option d is right because larger samples make t approach the normal shape. For example, with 5 students Meera needs a t of about 2.78 for significance at .05, while with 101 students about 1.98 is enough. Exam trap: 'broader' and 'flatter' describe the small-sample t, the reverse of what happens as n grows, and t is never skewed at any sample size.", "traps": {"a": "Broader (more spread) describes the t curve with few degrees of freedom; as sample size grows its spread shrinks toward that of the normal curve.", "b": "The t distribution is symmetrical around zero at every sample size; positive skew is a feature of distributions such as chi-square and F, not t.", "c": "Flatter (a lower peak with heavier tails) is how t looks with small samples; with larger samples it becomes taller in the centre and thinner in the tails, like the normal curve."}}, {"q": "Another expression of constant variance is:", "concept": "The answer is b: homoscedasticity is another expression for constant variance. The word comes from the Greek homo (same) and skedasis (scattering). In regression and correlation it means that the scatter of Y, or of the residuals (prediction errors), is about the same at every value of X; in group comparisons such as the t test and ANOVA the same idea is called homogeneity of variance, meaning equal variances across groups. It is a key assumption of these parametric methods and is checked with a residual plot or with tests such as those of Howard Levene (1960) and Maurice Bartlett (1937) or the F-max test of H. O. Hartley (1950). Its violation, heteroscedasticity, shows as a fan or funnel shape in which the errors widen as X grows, and it makes standard errors and significance tests untrustworthy. Option b is right because 'constant variance' is the literal meaning of homoscedasticity. For example, if Kabir predicts monthly spending from income and finds that spending varies little among low earners but hugely among high earners, his residual plot fans out: that is heteroscedasticity, not constant variance. Exam trap: homoscedasticity and heteroscedasticity are opposites that examiners swap; homo means same, hetero means different. Leptokurtosis describes the peak and tails of a single distribution, and covariance describes how two variables move together; neither is about equal spread.", "traps": {"a": "Leptokurtosis is a kurtosis term for a distribution more peaked, with heavier tails, than the normal curve; it describes the shape of one distribution, not equal variance across values or groups.", "c": "Heteroscedasticity is the opposite, unequal (non-constant) variance of residuals or groups, seen as a fan-shaped scatter; it is a violation of the assumption, not another name for it.", "d": "Covariance measures how two variables vary together (the average product of their deviations from their means) and is the raw basis of correlation; it says nothing about variance being constant."}}, {"q": "An orientation towards understanding mental processes such as perceiving, remembering, reasoning, deciding and problem solving and their relationship to behaviour is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: an orientation towards understanding mental processes such as perceiving, remembering, reasoning, deciding and problem solving, and how they relate to behaviour, is the cognitive perspective. A perspective is a broad viewpoint that decides which questions psychologists ask. The cognitive perspective treats the mind as an active processor of information: input is attended to, encoded, stored, retrieved and used to make decisions. It grew out of the cognitive revolution of the 1950s, when George Miller (1956) showed that short-term memory holds about seven items and Noam Chomsky (1959) argued that language cannot be explained by reinforcement alone. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the field in his book Cognitive Psychology. Because mental processes cannot be seen directly, researchers infer them from measurable data such as reaction times, recall scores and errors. Example: when Meera forgets a phone number because a friend interrupts her, a cognitive psychologist asks how much information working memory holds and why interference wipes it out. Option b is right because the stem lists exactly the processes that define this perspective and adds their link to behaviour. Exam trap: the behavioural perspective also studies behaviour, so examiners count on the shared word; but John B. Watson (1913) excluded mental processes from science as unobservable, and B. F. Skinner (1938) rejected them as causes of behaviour, so a viewpoint built around perceiving and reasoning cannot be behavioural.", "traps": {"a": "The behavioural perspective (John B. Watson, B. F. Skinner) studies observable behaviour and its environmental causes and does not explain behaviour by inner mental processes, so it does not centre on perceiving or reasoning.", "c": "The biological perspective explains behaviour through the brain, nervous system, genes and hormones; it may study the neural basis of memory, but its focus is physical mechanisms, not mental processes as such.", "d": "'Subjectivistic' is not a standard perspective; subjective, first-person experience is the focus of the humanistic and phenomenological approaches (Carl Rogers), not the scientific study of information processing."}}, {"q": "Condition in which factors other than independent variables are permitted to vary across experimental conditions, invalidating the results is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: when factors other than the independent variable are allowed to vary across experimental conditions, so that the results cannot be trusted, the experiment is confounded. In an experiment the researcher changes one thing, the independent variable (IV), and measures its effect on the dependent variable (DV). Every other factor should be held constant or spread evenly across groups by random assignment. A confounding variable is an extraneous variable that changes systematically along with the IV, so any difference in the DV could be due to either. The study then loses internal validity: you can no longer claim that the IV caused the effect. Example: Kabir tests whether music improves memory, but the music group learns in the morning and the silence group in the evening. Time of day is confounded with music, so a better score in the music group proves nothing. Controls include random assignment, matching, counterbalancing and keeping conditions identical. Option a is right because 'other factors permitted to vary across conditions, invalidating the results' is the textbook definition of confounding. Exam trap: the experimenter effect and the placebo effect are themselves specific sources of confounding, so they sound right; but the stem gives the general definition, and the general term is confounding.", "traps": {"b": "The experimenter effect (experimenter bias, studied by Robert Rosenthal, 1966) is when the researcher's expectations or behaviour influence participants' responses; it is one possible confound, not the general name for uncontrolled variation across conditions.", "c": "The placebo effect is improvement that comes from expecting a treatment rather than from the treatment itself; it is controlled with a placebo group and is only one specific source of confounding.", "d": "Deception means misleading participants about the true purpose or procedure of a study; it is an ethical issue, not a failure of experimental control."}}, {"q": "People's report of how they perceive an object to be and how bright a flash of light seems to be is an example of:", "concept": "The answer is b: asking people to report how an object looks to them, or how bright a flash of light seems, is introspection. Introspection means 'looking inward': a trained observer examines his or her own conscious experience of a stimulus and describes it. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) used it in the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig, but not as casual self-reflection. His observers saw controlled stimuli such as lights, tones and metronome beats and gave short, immediate judgements of their sensations and feelings. Edward Titchener, working at Cornell, developed a stricter version that he named structural psychology (1898), training observers to report only basic elements (sensations, images and feelings) and not to name the object itself, which he called the 'stimulus error'. Example: Riya watches two flashes in a laboratory and reports that the second seems brighter and slightly yellow; her report of her own experience is introspective data. Introspection lost ground because observers in different laboratories disagreed and private reports could not be checked; John B. Watson (1913) rejected it entirely. Option b is right because the stem describes people reporting their own perceptual experience. Exam trap: reports of how bright a light seems are also the raw material of psychophysics, founded by Gustav Fechner (1860), but psychophysics is not offered here; among the four options, only introspection is a method of self-report.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud's theory and therapy, which uses free association and dream analysis to uncover unconscious material, not reports of conscious sensations of lights and objects.", "c": "The cathartic effect is emotional release through expressing pent-up feelings, from Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's 'talking cure' (1895); it has nothing to do with judging brightness.", "d": "The law of effect is Edward Thorndike's principle, drawn from his puzzle-box studies of cats (1898), that responses followed by satisfying consequences are strengthened; it concerns learning from consequences, not reports of experience."}}, {"q": "Part of neuron that conducts action potential away from the cell body is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the axon is the part of a neuron that conducts the action potential away from the cell body. A typical neuron has three main parts. Dendrites are branching fibres that receive messages from other cells. The cell body (soma) contains the nucleus and adds up the incoming signals. The axon is a single long fibre that leaves the soma at the axon hillock. When the summed input reaches threshold, an action potential (a brief, all-or-none electrical impulse) starts near the hillock and travels down the axon to the axon terminals, which release neurotransmitter onto the next cell. Many axons are wrapped in a fatty myelin sheath broken by gaps called nodes of Ranvier, so the impulse jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction) and travels much faster. A simple memory aid: dendrites deliver signals to the cell, the axon carries them away. Option c is right because the axon is the only listed structure that carries the impulse away from the soma. Exam trap: dendrites carry graded signals the other way, towards the cell body, and examiners often swap 'towards' and 'away from' to catch students who learned the parts without the direction of flow.", "traps": {"a": "A synapse is the junction between one neuron's axon terminal and the next cell, where neurotransmitter crosses a tiny gap; Charles Sherrington named it in 1897. It is a meeting point, not the part of a neuron that conducts impulses.", "b": "Dendrites receive input from other neurons and carry graded potentials towards the cell body, the opposite direction to the one in the stem.", "d": "Glial cells are supporting cells, not parts of a neuron: oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells make myelin, astrocytes nourish neurons and microglia clear debris. They do not conduct action potentials."}}, {"q": "A form of conditioning in which the onset of the Unconditioned Stimulus (UCS) begins while the conditioned stimulus is still present is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: when the conditioned stimulus (CS) starts first and is still present when the unconditioned stimulus (UCS) begins, the arrangement is delay conditioning. In Ivan Pavlov's (1927) classical conditioning, learning depends heavily on the timing of the CS (for example, a tone) and the UCS (for example, food). In delay (delayed) conditioning, one of the forward arrangements, the CS comes on, stays on, and overlaps with the UCS. The 'delay' is the interval before the UCS arrives, not slowness of learning. It is usually the most effective arrangement, because the CS works as a warning signal that predicts the UCS. Example: in a laboratory, a tone sounds and keeps sounding until a puff of air hits Arjun's eye; after a number of pairings he blinks as soon as the tone comes on, before any puff arrives. Option b is right because the stem's key detail, that the UCS begins while the CS is still present, defines the overlap of delay conditioning. Exam trap: trace conditioning is also a forward arrangement (CS first), so students confuse the two; the difference is that in trace conditioning the CS ends before the UCS starts, leaving a gap that must be bridged by a memory 'trace', and learning is weaker.", "traps": {"a": "In trace conditioning the CS is switched off before the UCS begins, leaving a gap between them; the stem says the CS is still present, which rules it out.", "c": "In simultaneous conditioning the CS and UCS start at the same moment, so the CS gives no warning and learning is weak; the stem says the CS was already on when the UCS began.", "d": "In backward conditioning the UCS comes before the CS; it produces little or no excitatory conditioning and can even make the CS a signal that the UCS is over."}}, {"q": "The learning in which material to be learned is broken up into small easy steps is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: learning in which the material is broken up into small, easy steps is programmed learning (programmed instruction). B. F. Skinner (1954) argued in 'The Science of Learning and the Art of Teaching' that classrooms reinforce correct answers too rarely and too late. He applied operant principles to teaching: content is divided into small units called frames; the learner makes an active response to each frame; feedback, which acts as reinforcement, is immediate; and each learner moves at his or her own pace, with steps small enough that errors are rare. He delivered these programmes on teaching machines, building on the machine Sidney Pressey built in the 1920s. In a linear programme (Skinner) every learner follows the same sequence of frames; in a branching programme, developed by Norman Crowder in the late 1950s, a wrong answer sends the learner to a remedial frame. Example: Meera learns the parts of the eye from an app that shows one fact, asks one question, tells her at once whether she is right, and only then moves on. Option b is right because breaking material into small, easy steps is the defining feature of programmed learning. Exam trap: programmed learning is built on operant conditioning, so option a tempts; but operant conditioning is the general learning process (behaviour shaped by its consequences), while programmed learning is the teaching method that applies it.", "traps": {"a": "Operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner, is learning through the reinforcement and punishment of voluntary behaviour; programmed learning uses it, but the stem describes a teaching method of small steps, not the underlying learning process.", "c": "Social learning is Albert Bandura's (1977) observational learning, acquiring behaviour by watching models and the consequences they receive; it does not involve breaking material into graded steps.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because programmed learning (option b) names the method exactly."}}, {"q": "Multi-factor theory of intelligence by Thorndike is at one extreme of the interpretation regarding the nature of:", "concept": "The answer is c: Edward Thorndike's (1927) multifactor theory sits at one extreme of the interpretations regarding the nature of mental organisation, that is, how the abilities that make up intelligence are structured. Theorists who correlated many tests asked whether the abilities behind them form one general power, a few broad group factors or a host of separate bits. Thorndike gave the most splintered answer. For him intelligence is made up of a multitude of separate, minute elements of ability, each a set of stimulus-response connections. Any mental act uses a number of these elements together, two tasks correlate only to the extent that they share elements, and there is no general intelligence: a brighter person simply has more connections. The theory is therefore called atomistic. Even so, Thorndike grouped tasks into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence. At the other extreme stands Charles Spearman's (1904) two-factor theory, in which all intellectual activity depends mainly on one general factor, g, plus a specific factor, s, for each task; group-factor theories such as that of Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) sit in between. Example: Kabir repairs engines brilliantly but is average at abstract puzzles; Thorndike would call these largely separate abilities. Option c is right because the range from multifactor to two-factor theories is a range of views on how the mind's abilities are organised, which is exactly the phrase the textbook uses. Exam trap: the opposite pole is Spearman's g, not Thurstone's primary mental abilities, which are a middle position; and do not mix up Thorndike (multifactor) with Thurstone (primary mental abilities). Key note: the stem copies a sentence from Frank S. Freeman's Theory and Practice of Psychological Testing (third edition, 1962), which reads 'the nature of mental organization', so c is the answer the paper intends. Option a, intellectual organisation, means much the same thing here; if you picked it you understood the idea, but on this paper c is the safe mark, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Intellectual organisation' means much the same as mental organisation here, but the sentence this stem is copied from (Frank S. Freeman, 1962) says 'mental organization', so c is the intended answer (see the Key note).", "b": "Reasoning is one specific ability inside the structure (one of Louis Leon Thurstone's primary mental abilities), not the broad question of how intelligence as a whole is organised.", "d": "Motor organisation concerns the control and coordination of movement (motor development and the motor system), not the structure of intelligence."}}, {"q": "Who developed the concept of primary mental abilities?", "concept": "The answer is c: Louis Leon Thurstone (1938) developed the concept of primary mental abilities. Charles Spearman (1904) had argued that a single general factor, g, runs through all intellectual tasks. Thurstone gave a large battery of tests to college students and analysed the scores with his own method, multiple factor analysis. Instead of one g he found seven relatively independent group factors, which he called primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, associative memory, perceptual speed and reasoning (induction). He argued that a person is better described by a profile across these abilities than by a single IQ, and built tests to measure each one. Later analyses showed that the seven abilities correlate with one another, so a general factor reappeared at a higher level, a point Thurstone himself later accepted. Example: Arjun scores high on space and number but low on word fluency; Thurstone would report that uneven profile instead of averaging it into one score. Option c is right because 'primary mental abilities' is Thurstone's own term for his seven factors. Exam trap: all four names are factor theorists of intelligence, so the item tests matching each theorist to his model: g and s for Spearman, primary mental abilities for Thurstone, fluid and crystallised intelligence for Raymond B. Cattell (1963), structure of intellect for J. P. Guilford (1967), and abstract, mechanical and social intelligence for Edward Thorndike (1927).", "traps": {"a": "R. B. Cattell (Raymond B. Cattell, 1963, later with John Horn) split intelligence into fluid intelligence (reasoning with new problems) and crystallised intelligence (acquired knowledge); he did not propose primary mental abilities.", "b": "E. L. Thorndike (Edward Thorndike) proposed the multifactor theory, in which intelligence is many specific connections grouped into abstract, mechanical and social intelligence; primary mental abilities are not his.", "d": "J. P. Guilford's (1967) structure of intellect model crosses operations, contents and products to give 120 abilities (later 150 and then 180); it is a different and much larger model."}}, {"q": "The life goal of an individual is a/an:", "concept": "The answer is d: an individual's life goal is classed as a personal motive. Indian textbooks commonly sort motives into three groups. Biological (physiological or primary) motives are unlearned and rooted in bodily needs: hunger, thirst, sleep, sex and pain avoidance. Social (psychosocial) motives are learned through living in groups and are satisfied through other people: achievement, affiliation, power and approval. Personal motives belong to one individual and grow out of his or her own history: habits, interests, attitudes, level of aspiration and life goals. A life goal is personal because it is chosen and pursued by one person and differs widely between people, even within the same family or culture. Example: Riya and her brother grow up in the same home, but Riya's life goal is to become a clinical psychologist while her brother wants to run the family shop; each goal belongs to its owner. Option d is right because the standard list places life goals, along with level of aspiration and interests, among personal motives. Exam trap: a life goal sounds social because family and society shape it, and achievement is a social motive; but the classification asks whose motive it is, and a particular goal adopted by one person is personal. Key note: some books fold life goals and level of aspiration into social or psychosocial motives, since they are learned in a social setting, which makes c look possible. The more common three-way scheme (biological, social, personal) lists life goals under personal motives, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Biological motives are unlearned, body-based needs such as hunger, thirst and sleep; a life goal is learned and chosen, not a bodily need.", "b": "An instinct is an unlearned, fixed, species-typical pattern of behaviour (William McDougall's instinct theory, 1908); a life goal is learned and varies from person to person.", "c": "Social motives (achievement, affiliation, power, approval) are learned needs shared by members of a group and satisfied through others; a particular life goal is specific to one individual, so it is classed as personal."}}, {"q": "Self-actualizing tendency according to Maslow is a/an:", "concept": "The answer is d: for Abraham Maslow (1954), the self-actualising tendency is growth motivation. Maslow arranged human needs in a hierarchy: physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem and, at the top, self-actualisation, becoming everything one is capable of becoming. He split the motives behind these needs into two kinds. The four lower needs are deficiency needs (D-needs): a lack creates tension, the person acts to fill the gap, and once it is filled the need stops motivating, like hunger after a meal. Self-actualisation works differently. It is a growth or being need (B-need) that does not arise from a lack; satisfying it makes it stronger, not weaker, and it is never finished. In Toward a Psychology of Being (1962) Maslow contrasted this growth motivation with deficiency motivation; he later called the motivation of self-actualisers metamotivation, directed at being values such as truth, beauty and justice. Example: Kabir has a secure job, close friends and respect at work, yet he takes up painting simply to develop himself; nothing is missing, he is growing. Option d is right because self-actualisation is Maslow's model case of growth rather than deficiency motivation. Exam trap: Maslow did call human needs 'instinctoid', meaning weak, instinct-like tendencies that culture can easily override, which makes 'instinct' tempting; but instinctoid is not instinct, since instincts are fixed and unlearned, and the textbook label for self-actualisation is growth motivation.", "traps": {"a": "An instinct is an unlearned, fixed, species-typical behaviour pattern (William McDougall, 1908); Maslow described needs only as 'instinctoid' (weakly instinct-like), and he did not call self-actualisation an instinct.", "b": "Deficiency motivation drives the four lower needs (physiological, safety, belongingness, esteem), which arise from a lack and stop once met; self-actualisation is Maslow's contrasting case.", "c": "Imprinting is rapid attachment learning in a critical period early in life, shown by Konrad Lorenz (1935) with goslings that followed him; it has nothing to do with Maslow's needs."}}, {"q": "According to James-Lange theory, what among the following informs the brain that it is experiencing emotions?", "concept": "The answer is c: according to the James-Lange theory, the brain learns that it is experiencing an emotion from physiological feedback from the body. William James (1884) and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) proposed the idea independently. Common sense says we see a bear, feel afraid and then tremble. James reversed the order: the stimulus triggers bodily changes first (racing heart, trembling, fast breathing, tense muscles), and the emotion is our perception of those changes. In his words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Without the bodily response, he argued, the perception would stay a pale, purely intellectual judgement with no emotional warmth. Each emotion would therefore need its own pattern of bodily response. Example: Arjun's motorbike skids on a wet road; his heart pounds and his hands shake before he has time to think, and only as he notices this pounding does he feel fear. Later facial feedback research, in which holding a smile slightly lifts mood, gives the theory partial support. Option c is right because, for James and Lange, the feeling is built from signals fed back to the brain from the viscera and muscles. Exam trap: option d describes the first step, seeing the stimulus, which every theory shares; the James-Lange point is that seeing alone does not create the emotion, while the body's response, fed back to the brain, does.", "traps": {"a": "The hypothalamus organises emotional arousal and rage (Philip Bard's sham rage work in cats, 1928); brain-centred accounts such as the Cannon-Bard theory, which stressed the thalamus, were rivals to James-Lange, which locates the source of felt emotion in bodily feedback.", "b": "The amygdala evaluates threat and drives fear responses (Joseph LeDoux, 1996); it belongs to later neural accounts of emotion, not to the James-Lange theory, which names no specific brain structure as the source of feeling.", "d": "Seeing the emotional stimulus is the first step in every theory, but James and Lange held that perception alone gives only a pale, emotionless judgement; the emotion arrives when the bodily response is felt."}}, {"q": "When Walter Cannon removed the sympathetic nervous system of the cat what did he find?", "concept": "The answer is c: when Walter Cannon removed the sympathetic nervous system of cats, the cats still showed emotional behaviour such as anger, fear and pleasure. The James-Lange theory says that an emotion is the perception of bodily (visceral) changes, so cutting the viscera off from the brain should abolish it. Walter Cannon, with J. T. Lewis and S. W. Britton (1927), kept cats healthy for many months after removing the entire sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system, the division that acts in great excitement. These cats could no longer make sympathetic blood-vessel responses, release adrenaline from the adrenal medulla, mobilise sugar from the liver or erect their hair. Yet faced with a barking dog they showed all the superficial signs of rage: hissing, growling, ears drawn back, teeth bared and a paw lifted to strike; only the bristling fur was missing. In his 1927 critique of James-Lange, Cannon set these cats beside Charles Sherrington's (1900) dogs, whose viscera had been cut off from the brain, and concluded that the operated animals behaved 'as angrily, as joyfully, as fearfully as ever'. This was the first of his five criticisms; the others were that the same visceral changes occur in different emotions and in non-emotional states, the viscera are too insensitive, visceral changes are too slow, and injected adrenaline produces arousal without true emotion. Cannon proposed instead a thalamic theory, extended by Philip Bard (1928) and now called the Cannon-Bard theory: the thalamus signals the cortex and the body at the same time. Example: on this view, if Meera's heart could not race, she would still feel angry when insulted. Option c is right because emotional behaviour survived without sympathetic feedback, which the James-Lange theory could not predict. Exam trap: option b reverses the finding; sympathetic arousal is exactly what the surgery removed, so the striking result was emotion without arousal.", "traps": {"a": "The cats did not fall into a coma or die; Walter Cannon's team kept them healthy for many months in the laboratory, which is why their emotional behaviour could be observed at all.", "b": "Sympathetic arousal, such as blood-vessel changes, adrenaline release and bristling fur, is exactly what removing the sympathetic division abolished; the surprise was that emotional behaviour remained without it.", "d": "The split between predatory (quiet biting) attack and affective (hissing, defensive) attack comes from later hypothalamic stimulation studies in cats, such as John Flynn's in the 1960s, not from Cannon's sympathectomy."}}, {"q": "Timbre is the quality of sound resulting from:", "concept": "The answer is a: timbre is the quality of a sound that comes from the complexity of the sound wave. Hearing turns three physical properties of a sound wave into three experiences. Frequency, the number of cycles per second measured in hertz (Hz), is heard as pitch; humans hear about 20 to 20,000 Hz. Amplitude, the height of the wave, is heard as loudness, measured in decibels. Complexity, the mixture of frequencies in the wave, is heard as timbre, or tone quality. Almost no natural sound is a pure tone. A musical note has a fundamental frequency, which sets its pitch, plus overtones (harmonics) at multiples of that frequency. Each instrument or voice produces its own blend of overtones, which gives its wave a distinctive shape. Example: Meera plays a note on a sitar and Kabir plays the same note on a flute at the same loudness; pitch and loudness match, yet anyone can tell the two instruments apart, because the overtone mix, the timbre, differs. Option a is right because timbre depends on the complexity of the waveform. Exam trap: frequency (option b) is the commonest wrong pick, since pitch and timbre both sound like 'quality' of a note; frequency gives pitch only, and loudness comes from amplitude.", "traps": {"b": "The frequency of a sound wave determines pitch (how high or low a note sounds), not timbre; two instruments can match in frequency and still differ in timbre.", "c": "Loudness is the experience of a wave's amplitude, measured in decibels; it is a separate dimension from timbre, which stays recognisable at any volume.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option a, complexity of the sound wave, is the standard definition of what produces timbre."}}, {"q": "Which among the following is an underlying mechanism of perceptual constancy?", "concept": "The answer is c: perceptual constancy rests on top-down processing. Perceptual constancy is the tendency to see objects as keeping the same size, shape, brightness and colour even though the image on the retina keeps changing. A friend walking away casts a smaller and smaller retinal image, yet she does not seem to shrink. A door swinging open projects a trapezoid, yet we see a rectangle. The brain achieves this by bringing knowledge and context to the raw input: the object's familiar size, how far away it seems, the angle of view and the surrounding light. Hermann von Helmholtz (1867) called this unconscious inference. Using prior knowledge and expectation to interpret sensory data is top-down (conceptually driven) processing. Example: Riya sees her father at the far end of a railway platform; his retinal image is tiny, but because she knows his height and judges the distance, he looks his normal size. Option c is right because constancy depends on knowledge, expectation and context, not only on the features striking the retina. Exam trap: option d is a disguised definition of bottom-up processing (building perception from low-level features), placed beside option a so that bottom-up seems to be offered twice. Key note: some constancy cues, such as the ratio of an object's brightness to its surroundings, are computed early and partly bottom-up, so the mechanism is not purely top-down; but the standard textbook explanation stresses knowledge and inference, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Bottom-up processing builds a percept upwards from raw sensory features; on its own it would follow the changing retinal image, so it cannot explain why objects look constant.", "b": "A misleading perception that distorts or misjudges a stimulus is an illusion; constancy is usually accurate perception, although misapplied constancy cues can produce illusions such as the moon illusion.", "d": "Organising perception by beginning with low-level features is a description of bottom-up processing, the same idea as option a in different words."}}, {"q": "Among the following which would present the greatest loss to the professional chef?", "concept": "The answer is b: an olfactory loss (loss of the sense of smell) would hurt a professional chef the most. What we call the taste of food is really flavour, and flavour is taste plus smell. The taste buds on the tongue detect only five basic tastes: sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami. Everything else, such as the difference between cardamom and cinnamon, fresh and stale ghee, or mango and banana, comes from odour molecules that travel from the mouth up the back of the nose to the olfactory epithelium (retronasal smell). People who lose smell (anosmia), after a head injury or a viral infection such as COVID-19, often say food has 'lost its taste', even though their taste buds still work. A chef makes fine flavour judgements all day, so losing smell would cripple the job. Example: Arjun, a chef, has a heavy cold; he can tell that a curry is salty, but he cannot tell whether he added too much garam masala. Option b is right because smell supplies most of the flavour a chef must judge. Exam trap: students look for a taste option, and when none is offered they may pick a sense that sounds more serious, such as hearing; the sense that matters most for flavour is smell.", "traps": {"a": "Vestibular loss (damage to the semicircular canals and otolith organs of the inner ear) disturbs balance and causes dizziness; it does not affect flavour, the chef's core skill.", "c": "Kinaesthetic loss removes the sense of the position and movement of the limbs (receptors in muscles, tendons and joints); it would make chopping clumsy but leaves flavour judgement intact.", "d": "Injury to the cochlea causes sensorineural hearing loss; hearing matters little when judging food."}}, {"q": "Inductive reasoning goes from specific to the general. Which among the following is analogous to inductive reasoning?", "concept": "The answer is b: bottom-up processing is analogous to inductive reasoning. Inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to a general conclusion: Kabir sees that every crow he has met is black and concludes that crows are black. Deductive reasoning moves the other way, from a general rule to a specific case. Perception can run in the same two directions. In bottom-up (data-driven) processing, the brain starts with small sensory features, such as lines, edges, angles and colours picked up by feature detector cells, and builds them up into a whole object. The direct perception theory of James J. Gibson (1966) is the classic bottom-up account. In top-down (conceptually driven) processing, knowledge, expectations and context guide how the features are read, as when you make out a badly written word correctly because of the sentence around it. Option b is right because both inductive reasoning and bottom-up processing build the general (a conclusion, a whole percept) from particulars (single observations, single features). Exam trap: top-down processing is the parallel of deductive reasoning, from general knowledge down to a specific interpretation; reversing the pairing is the trap, so remember that bottom-up and inductive both climb from parts to whole.", "traps": {"a": "Top-down processing works from general knowledge and expectations down to the interpretation of specific input, so it parallels deductive, not inductive, reasoning.", "c": "Perceptual constancies (size, shape, brightness, colour) are outcomes of perception, seeing objects as stable, not a direction of processing; they rely mainly on top-down knowledge.", "d": "Illusions are misperceptions of real stimuli (such as the moon illusion); they are phenomena that reveal how perception works, not a specific-to-general process."}}, {"q": "Which measure is directly related to the variance and is figured by taking the square root of variance?", "concept": "The answer is c: the standard deviation (SD) is the measure obtained by taking the square root of the variance. Both describe how spread out scores are around the mean. To find the variance, take each score's deviation from the mean, square it so that negative and positive deviations do not cancel, and average the squares: for a population, variance = Σ(X minus M)² ÷ N. Squaring leaves the result in squared units (marks squared), which is hard to interpret. Taking the square root returns it to the original units, giving the SD, the typical distance of a score from the mean. Karl Pearson (1894) introduced the name 'standard deviation'. Example: Meera's class scores 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7 and 9 on a quiz. The mean is 5, the squared deviations add up to 32, the variance is 32 ÷ 8 = 4, and the SD is the square root of 4, which is 2 marks. Because it uses every score and ties in with the normal curve (about 68 per cent of scores fall within 1 SD of the mean), the SD is the most widely used measure of dispersion. Option c is right because the SD is defined as the square root of the variance. Exam trap: the average (mean) deviation also measures distance from the mean, but it averages absolute deviations instead of squaring them, so it is not derived from the variance.", "traps": {"a": "Average (mean) deviation is the mean of the absolute deviations from the mean, Σ|X minus M| ÷ N; it ignores signs instead of squaring, so it is not the square root of the variance.", "b": "Quartile deviation (semi-interquartile range) is half the distance between the third and first quartiles, (Q3 minus Q1) ÷ 2; it uses only the middle 50 per cent of scores and is not based on the variance.", "d": "Range is simply the highest score minus the lowest; it depends only on the two extreme scores and has no link to the variance."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a characteristic of the mean?", "concept": "The answer is d: 'it is best used with ordinal data' is not a characteristic of the mean. The arithmetic mean (M = ΣX ÷ N) is the sum of the scores divided by their number. It has three classic properties. First, it is the balance point of the distribution: deviations above and below it cancel, so the sum of deviations about the mean is always zero. Second, the sum of squared deviations about the mean is smaller than about any other value, the least-squares property that makes the mean the basis of variance, SD and regression. Third, because it uses every score, it is affected by extreme scores: the marks 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8, but adding one score of 40 lifts it to about 13.3. The mean assumes equal distances between scores, so it needs interval or ratio data. Ordinal data, such as class ranks or finishing positions in a race, show only order; the gap between first and second need not equal the gap between second and third, so the median is the proper average, and for nominal data the mode. Example: if Arjun's teacher records only class ranks, the median rank is a fair summary, but a mean rank assumes that the gap between 1st and 2nd equals the gap between 9th and 10th, which ranks cannot tell us. Option d is right because ordinal data suit the median, not the mean. Exam trap: the item asks for what is NOT true; options a, b and c are all genuine properties, so a hurried reader picks the first true statement.", "traps": {"a": "Being pulled by extreme scores is a true characteristic of the mean, and its main weakness; that is why the median is preferred for skewed data.", "b": "Minimising the sum of squared deviations (the least-squares property) is a true characteristic of the mean.", "c": "The sum of deviations about the mean is always zero; this is a true, defining property of the mean as the balance point of the distribution."}}, {"q": "Ratios measured by absolute variation for each observation are considered as:", "concept": "The answer is b: ratios formed from absolute variation are relative measures of dispersion. Measures of dispersion come in two kinds. Absolute measures (range, quartile deviation, mean deviation and standard deviation) are expressed in the same units as the data: marks, centimetres, rupees. They show how spread out one set of data is, but they cannot fairly compare sets measured in different units, or sets with very different averages. Relative measures solve this by dividing an absolute measure by an average or a related value, which cancels the units and leaves a pure number or percentage. Each absolute measure has a relative partner: coefficient of range = (highest minus lowest) ÷ (highest plus lowest); coefficient of quartile deviation = (Q3 minus Q1) ÷ (Q3 plus Q1); coefficient of mean deviation = mean deviation ÷ the average it was taken from; and coefficient of variation = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Example: Kabir's shop averages Rs 5,000 a day in sales with an SD of Rs 500, while his sister's averages Rs 20,000 with an SD of Rs 2,000; both coefficients of variation are 10 per cent, so their relative variability is the same. Option b is right because a ratio built from absolute variation is, by definition, a relative measure. Exam trap: 'non-relative' (option a) tempts because the stem mentions absolute variation, but the absolute measure is only the raw ingredient; once it is turned into a ratio, it becomes relative. Key note: the stem is garbled as printed ('ratios measured by absolute variation for each observation'); read as ratios of absolute variation to an average, it describes relative measures, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "'Non-relative measures' is not a standard term; the absolute measures (range, quartile deviation, mean deviation, SD) stay in the data's units, whereas a ratio formed from them is relative.", "c": "'High uniform measures' is not a statistical term; uniformity (consistency) is judged with a relative measure such as the coefficient of variation, where a lower value means more uniform data.", "d": "'Low uniform measures' is a filler with no meaning in statistics; it echoes the idea of consistency but names no class of dispersion measures."}}, {"q": "_____ is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even though they are measured in different units.", "concept": "The answer is c: the coefficient of variation (CV) is used to compare the dispersion of two or more sets of data even when they are measured in different units. Karl Pearson (1896) defined it as the standard deviation expressed as a percentage of the mean: CV = (SD ÷ mean) × 100. Because the SD and the mean are in the same units, the units cancel and the CV is a pure percentage. That allows comparisons the SD cannot make: heights in centimetres against weights in kilograms, or the spread of marks in a class whose mean is 30 against one whose mean is 80. The set with the lower CV is the more consistent (more uniform, less variable). Example: in Riya's class, height has a mean of 160 cm and an SD of 8 cm (CV = 5 per cent), while weight has a mean of 50 kg and an SD of 5 kg (CV = 10 per cent); weight varies relatively twice as much, even though 8 looks larger than 5. Because it divides by the mean, the CV strictly needs ratio data with a true zero. Option c is right because it is the standard relative, unit-free measure of dispersion. Exam trap: the standard deviation is the best-known measure of spread, so it attracts guesses; but it is stated in the original units, so comparing an SD in centimetres with one in kilograms is meaningless.", "traps": {"a": "Range (highest minus lowest score) is an absolute measure in the original units and depends on just two extreme scores, so it cannot compare data measured in different units.", "b": "The standard deviation is the most used measure of spread, but it is in the original units (cm, kg), so SDs of differently measured variables cannot be compared directly; dividing it by the mean gives the CV.", "d": "Mean deviation, the average absolute distance of scores from the mean, is also in the original units; its unit-free form is the coefficient of mean deviation, not mean deviation itself."}}]});
